export const docsIndexIntro = "Creora is a private AI workspace where a conversation becomes a working product. You can chat with a private AI, make pictures and videos, and build applications you own, and all of it happens inside one platform that you never have to leave.";

export const docsPages: Record<string, string> = {
  "account": "Go further\n\nYour account\n\nEverything about you, rather than about one project, lives in Settings. Open it from the account menu on your avatar, and the sections run down the left or sit in a chooser at the top on a phone.\n\nSettings sections\n\n  \n\nAccount\n\nYour username, an optional notification email and your wallet address with a button to copy it. The notification email is only used to tell you about your account, such as a suspended site or credits running out, and it can never sign you in or recover the account.\n\n  \n\nProfile\n\nA display name of up to 50 characters, a bio of up to 300, up to five https links, a profile picture (up to 1 MB) and a banner (up to 4 MB). \nShow my wallet address\n is off by default, and \nHide my profile\n makes your page read as not found to everyone else without unpublishing anything.\n\n  \n\nAPI key\n\nYour own Anthropic key, so builder runs on Claude are billed to you by Anthropic instead of costing credits. Covered in \nCredits and API key\n.\n\n  \n\nDevelopers\n\nWhere you make and revoke keys for the Creora API, up to ten at once. A key is shown once, when you make it. See \nAPI overview\n.\n\n  \n\nStorage\n\nWhere your uploaded images are kept, including the Filecoin option described below.\n\n  \n\nAppearance\n\nLight, Dark or System. Creora is dark until you choose otherwise. It applies immediately and is saved per device rather than per account.\n\n  \n\nBilling\n\nToday's free uses, your credit balance and usage, the plans and top ups, your deposit address and your payments.\n\n  \n\nGitHub\n\nThe token for importing and pushing repositories. See \nGitHub\n.\n\n  \n\nDanger zone\n\nAccount deletion.\n\nYour username\n\nChoose it in \nSettings › Account\n. It is 3 to 32 characters of lowercase letters, numbers, hyphens and underscores, and names that read alike count as the same name, so \nnick\n and \nn1ck\n cannot be two people. Some names are reserved.\n\nYour username is part of the address of everything you publish, so changing it moves your published pages and your profile, and old links stop working. A name you give up, by renaming or by deleting your account, is held for 30 days: you can take it back in that time, and to anybody else it reads as taken. A name Creora generated for you is never held.\n\nStoring on Filecoin\n\nKeep a copy of my uploads on Filecoin\n stores a copy of each profile picture, token picture and project asset with two independent storage providers. Each provider keeps the data under contract, paid for by Creora, and must prove on chain every day that it still holds it or lose its deposit. Creora keeps its own copy too, so images always load from Creora and work from the moment they are uploaded.\n\nA fresh upload shows \nStoring on Filecoin\n for a few minutes and then \nOn Filecoin\n. Each stored picture shows its piece identifier, the providers holding it, when their next proof is due, links to the PDP explorer where anybody can check the proofs, and an IPFS address that opens the image in any browser.\n\nThe switch decides what happens to your next upload, and turning it off later leaves the copies already made where they are. The assets pane in the editor puts the same switch on each upload.\n\nYour wallet is your way in\n\nYour account is your wallet address. There is no password and no recovery, because there is nothing to reset, and nobody at Creora can let you back in without the wallet. Signing in with Google, another social account or an email address does not change this: Reown makes a wallet for it, and whoever can sign in to that account or read that inbox can sign in as you, so guard it as you would a wallet.\n\nSigning in costs nothing and moves nothing. It is a message your wallet signs, never a transaction. See \nPrivacy and your wallet\n.\n\nDeleting your account\n\nOpen \nSettings › Danger zone\n, press \nDelete account\n and type your username to confirm. This removes your account, projects, files, messages, profile, pictures, published pages and credits, and published sites stop answering immediately. Forum posts are kept without your name so other people's conversations stay readable, and your username is held for 30 days. It cannot be undone, so download anything you want to keep first.\n\nSigning out\n\nChoose \nSign out\n from the account menu. The session is cleared and the wallet disconnected, and nothing of yours is deleted, so signing in with the same wallet brings everything back. You may also be signed out on your other devices after you add or remove an Anthropic key; signing in again is all it takes.\n\nIf something goes wrong\n\n  \n\n\"That username is taken.\"\n Somebody has it, or it is on hold for 30 days after being given up.\n\n  \n\n\"That username is reserved.\"\n Creora keeps that name for itself.\n\n  \n\nA picture is refused.\n Profile pictures and banners must be PNG, JPEG or WebP, a profile picture at most 1 MB and a banner at most 4 MB.",
  "agents": "Own and earn\n\nAgents\n\nAn agent is an AI that works for you on its own. You give it instructions and the tools it may use, then run it now or on a schedule. It searches the web, reads pages, makes pictures and videos, works with your apps' data and keeps notes from one run to the next, and it shows you every step it took and what each one cost.\n\nYour own agents, today.\n Every account can make agents and run them on its credits. Publishing an agent so other people can use it, and earning from it, is planned and not available yet; see \nComing\n.\n\nMaking an agent\n\n  \n\nOpen Agents\n\nPress \nAgents\n in the sidebar. Signed in, it opens \nyour agents\n. Signed out, it says what agents are and asks you to sign in first, because agents belong to accounts.\n\n  \n\nPress New agent\n\nUnder \nAbout\n, give it a \nName\n, which the agent knows too, and a \nDescription\n if you like, which is only for you, to tell your agents apart.\n\n  \n\nWrite its instructions\n\nInstructions\n say how it should work: what to look for, how to answer, and what it must never do. It reads them on every run. The \nStanding task\n is what it does on each scheduled run, and when you press \nRun now\n without giving it a task.\n\n  \n\nChoose its tools and apps\n\nTurn on only the tools it needs, and give it the apps whose data it may use. It is offered only the tools that are on.\n\n  \n\nSet a schedule and a cap\n\nLeave the schedule off to run it only when you press \nRun now\n, or pick a time for it to run on its own. \nMost credits a run can use\n is its cap. Press \nSave\n, and the agent's page opens.\n\nAn agent's page has three tabs. \nRuns\n is where you run it and read what it did, \nSettings\n holds everything above and \nDelete agent\n, and \nNotes\n shows what it remembers. Deleting an agent deletes its notes and its runs with it, and stops a run that is going. An account can have up to 10 agents.\n\nTools\n\nA new agent starts with \nSearch the web\n, \nRead pages\n and \nKeep notes\n on, and with pictures and videos off. Turn any of them on or off in \nSettings\n.\n\n  \n\nSearch the web\n\nLooks things up on the web and reads the results: up to ten sources, each with its title, address, date and a short extract.\n\n  \n\nRead pages\n\nOpens a public web page over https and reads its title and text. Creora's server fetches the page, and only from the public internet.\n\n  \n\nMake pictures\n\nMakes up to 4 pictures a run, the way the chat makes them. They show in the run.\n\n  \n\nMake videos\n\nStarts at most 1 video a run, the way the chat makes them. The run does not wait for it: it finishes on its own, and you watch it by opening the run again.\n\n  \n\nKeep notes\n\nKeeps and drops its own notes, to carry what it learns from one run to the next. See \nNotes\n.\n\n  \n\nYour apps' data\n\nUnder \nApps\n, give it up to 10 of your apps, each to \nRead\n, or to \nRead and write\n. It reads up to 100 rows at a time, adds up to 25 at a time and changes one row at a time.\n\nAn agent never deletes rows and never changes an app's tables, whatever it is given. An app you delete is taken from every agent it was given to.\n\nRunning it\n\nOn the \nRuns\n tab, type a task for this run and press \nRun now\n, or leave the box empty to use the standing task. The run opens under \nLatest run\n and each step shows as it happens: what it searched for or read, whether it worked, what it cost and how long it took. When it is done you see its answer, and any pictures and videos it made.\n\nPress \nStop\n at any time. The run stops within seconds, and you pay only for what it did. Up to 3 runs can go at once on an account.\n\nA run works in turns. In each turn the AI either asks for tools or gives its answer, and it can ask for several tools in one turn. A run is bounded:\n\n  \n\n12\n\nturns\n\n  \n\n20\n\ntool uses\n\n  \n\n5\n\nminutes\n\n  \n\n200\n\ncredits, its cap unless you set another\n\nClose to any of these, its tools are taken away and it is told to give its answer with what it has, and the run says which limit it reached, such as \nStopped at its credit cap\n. A tool that fails, such as a page that took too long, is shown as a failed step, the agent is told why, and the run goes on.\n\nHistory\n lists the newest 50 runs. Press one to open its task, steps, answer, pictures and videos. Each run is kept for 30 days.\n\nSchedules\n\nUnder \nSchedule and cap\n, \nRuns on its own\n is one of these:\n\n  \n\nOff\n\nIt runs only when you press \nRun now\n.\n\n  \n\nEvery hour\n\nAt the minutes past the hour you pick.\n\n  \n\nEvery day\n\nAt the time you pick.\n\n  \n\nEvery week\n\nOn the day and at the time you pick.\n\nTimes are shown in your own time zone and kept in UTC, so a schedule moves by an hour, in your time, when the clocks change. A scheduled run does the standing task, so a schedule needs one. Under the schedule, the form says the most it could spend in a day, such as \nUp to 4,800 credits a day\n for a 200 credit cap every hour.\n\nA scheduled run that cannot start, because the account lacks the credits for its cap or already has 3 runs going, is kept in the history as \nSkipped\n. After 3 runs in a row could not start for lack of credits, the schedule pauses itself and the agent's page says why. Once you have credits, press \nTurn the schedule on again\n.\n\nNotes\n\nNotes are what an agent remembers between runs. It reads every note on every run, and when \nKeep notes\n is on it keeps new ones and drops old ones itself. An agent keeps up to 50 notes of up to 500 characters each.\n\nThe \nNotes\n tab shows them all. You can add your own with \nAdd note\n, and edit or delete any of them, the agent's included. A note you write is a good place for something it should always know, such as who reads its answers and when.\n\nWhat a run costs\n\nAgents are paid in credits only, never with the free daily uses. When a run starts, its cap is held from your credits, and when it ends you are charged what it used and the rest is given back. A run never spends past its cap.\n\n  \n\nEach turn\n\nThe chat's rate: 260 credits per million tokens in and 800 per million out. Each turn reads the whole run so far again, so a run with many steps or long pages costs more than a short one.\n\n  \n\nA search\n\n16 credits, plus a little for reading the results.\n\n  \n\nA page\n\nNothing itself. Its text is read in the turns after it, as tokens.\n\n  \n\nA picture or a video\n\nThe chat's prices, as on \nWhat things cost\n. A picture or video that is refused costs nothing.\n\n  \n\nApps' data and notes\n\nNothing.\n\nMost credits a run can use\n is 200 unless you set another, from 20 to 5,000. A run that cannot have its cap held does not start, and says how much it needs. Every run shows what it spent of its cap, and every step what it cost.\n\nA cap must leave room for the paid tools that are on, or they could never be used: at least 40 credits for \nSearch the web\n or \nMake pictures\n, and at least 400 for \nMake videos\n. The form says what the tools you turned on need, under the cap, and will not save a cap smaller than that.\n\nWhat keeps it in bounds\n\nAn agent reads pages and search results that anybody could have written, so what it can do is set by your choices, not by its judgment.\n\n  \nIt uses only the tools that are on, reaches only the apps you gave it, and writes only to those given to read and write.\n\n  \nIt spends only up to its cap, and stays within the limits of a run.\n\n  \nEverything a page or a search result says is information to it, never instructions.\n\n  \nIt reads only the public internet, over https. Creora's server fetches each page, and refuses any address that leads to a private network.\n\n  \nIt holds the same two limits as the chat: nothing sexual involving someone under 18, and no sexual pictures or videos of a real person. See \nWhat the chat will not make\n.\n\nWhat a run sends to the AI, and what Creora keeps, is on \nPrivacy and your wallet\n. Every limit is on \nLimits reference\n.\n\nIf something goes wrong\n\n  \n\n\"This run needs up to 200 credits, and you have 120.\"\n Add credits in \nSettings › Billing\n, or lower the agent's cap.\n\n  \n\n\"Three runs are already going on this account. Wait for one to finish.\"\n Wait, or stop one of them.\n\n  \n\n\"Give this run a task, or set a standing task.\"\n Type a task in the box, or give the agent a standing task in \nSettings\n.\n\n  \n\n\"A schedule needs a standing task for its runs.\"\n Write a standing task before you turn a schedule on.\n\n  \n\n\"A video needs a cap of at least 400 credits.\"\n The cap is too small for a tool that is on. Raise the cap to what it says, or turn that tool off. A search or a picture needs at least 40.\n\n  \n\n\"This run reached its five minutes before it could answer.\"\n Its work took the whole five minutes. Give it a narrower task, or fewer pages to read.\n\n  \n\n\"This run was cut short by a restart. It was charged only for what it did.\"\n Creora restarted while it ran. Run it again.\n\n  \n\n\"This schedule was paused because three runs in a row could not start for lack of credits.\"\n Add credits, then press \nTurn the schedule on again\n.\n\n  \n\n\"You have 10 agents, the most an account can hold.\"\n Delete one to make another.\n\nComing\n\nPlanned\n\nNone of this is available yet. It is what comes next for agents, and this page will say when it is live.\n\n  \n\nPublishing an agent\n\nOn Starter or Pro, you will be able to publish an agent so anybody signed in can run it, for a price per run that you set.\n\n  \n\nEarning from it\n\nYou will earn 70% of that price, paid daily in USDG on Robinhood Chain, together with any \nGPU network\n earnings.\n\n  \n\nAgents through the API\n\nStarting a run, reading it and stopping it from your own code with the \nCreora API\n.\n\n  \n\nAgents calling agents\n\nAn agent able to call published agents you choose for it.\n\n  \n\nAn identity on chain\n\nA published agent registered in the ERC 8004 identity registry on Robinhood Chain, with the token sent to your wallet.",
  "api": "API\n\nAPI overview\n\nCreora's private AI from your own code: chat, pictures and videos over HTTPS. The API speaks OpenAI's language, so the official OpenAI libraries for Python and JavaScript work with it as they are. Point them at Creora, give them a Creora key, and every call is paid in credits.\n\n  \n\n7\n\nendpoints under one base URL\n\n  \n\n3\n\nmodels: chat, image and video\n\n  \n\n16\n\ncredits for a picture\n\n  \n\n~256\n\ncredits for a five second video\n\nBase URL\n\nEvery request goes to one address, over HTTPS:\n\nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1\n\nOpenAI compatible\n\nThe paths on these pages are relative to it, so \n/chat/completions\n means \nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1/chat/completions\n. \nSettings › Developers\n shows the same address with a button to copy it.\n\nGet a key\n\n  \n\nOpen Developers\n\nSign in and open \nSettings › Developers\n. The card there is called \nAPI keys\n.\n\n  \n\nName the key\n\nType a name that says where the key will be used, such as \nProduction server\n, in up to 60 characters, and press \nCreate key\n.\n\n  \n\nCopy it now\n\nYour new key appears once, with a \nCopy\n button. It starts with \ncrk_\n and is 44 characters long. Creora stores only a hash of it, so it can never show it to you again.\n\n  \n\nKeep it on your server\n\nPut it in an environment variable, such as \nCREORA_API_KEY\n, rather than in your code. Every example in these pages reads it from there.\n\nexport\n CREORA_API_KEY=\n\"crk_...\"\n\nThe list on the same card shows each key by its name and its last four characters, such as \ncrk_…a1B2\n, with when it was made and when it was last used (updated at most once a minute). An account can hold ten live keys at once; at ten, the form says so until you revoke one.\n\nAuthentication\n\nSend the key as a bearer token in the \nAuthorization\n header of every request. Nothing else opens the API: it never reads a cookie, so being signed in to creora.ai in a browser does not let a page call it.\n\nPOST /api/v1/chat/completions HTTP/1.1\nHost: creora.ai\nAuthorization: Bearer crk_...\nContent-Type: application/json\n\nA request without the header is refused with \n401\n and the code \nmissing_api_key\n. A key that is mistyped, malformed or revoked gets \n401\n and \ninvalid_api_key\n. Every error is described on \nErrors, limits and pricing\n.\n\nQuickstart\n\nInstall the official OpenAI library for your language. cURL needs nothing installed.\n\n  \n\ncURL\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\n# Nothing to install: check that curl is there\n\ncurl --version\n\n  \n\npip install openai\n\n  \n\nnpm install openai\n\nThen ask for your first answer. The only changes from code written for OpenAI are the base URL, the key and the model name.\n\n  \n\ncURL\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\ncurl https://creora.ai/api/v1/chat/completions \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n \\\n  -H \n\"Content-Type: application/json\"\n \\\n  -d \n'{\n    \"model\": \"creora-chat\",\n    \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Write a haiku about the sea.\"}]\n  }'\n\n  \n\nimport\n os\n\nfrom\n openai \nimport\n OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(\n    base_url=\n\"https://creora.ai/api/v1\"\n,\n    api_key=os.environ[\n\"CREORA_API_KEY\"\n],\n)\n\ncompletion = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\n\"creora-chat\"\n,\n    messages=[{\n\"role\"\n: \n\"user\"\n, \n\"content\"\n: \n\"Write a haiku about the sea.\"\n}],\n)\n\nprint\n(completion.choices[\n0\n].message.content)\n\n  \n\n// Node 18 or later, as an ES module (for example quickstart.mjs)\n\nimport\n \nOpenAI\n \nfrom\n \n'openai'\n\nconst\n client = \nnew\n \nOpenAI\n({\n  \nbaseURL\n: \n'https://creora.ai/api/v1'\n,\n  \napiKey\n: process.\nenv\n.\nCREORA_API_KEY\n,\n})\n\nconst\n completion = \nawait\n client.\nchat\n.\ncompletions\n.\ncreate\n({\n  \nmodel\n: \n'creora-chat'\n,\n  \nmessages\n: [{ \nrole\n: \n'user'\n, \ncontent\n: \n'Write a haiku about the sea.'\n }],\n})\n\nconsole\n.\nlog\n(completion.\nchoices\n[\n0\n].\nmessage\n.\ncontent\n)\n\nThe answer comes back in OpenAI's shape. Your ids, times, words and counts will differ:\n\n{\n\n  \n\"id\"\n\n:\n \n\"chatcmpl-9b1e6f0a2c4d8e7f13a5b6c9\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"object\"\n\n:\n \n\"chat.completion\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"created\"\n\n:\n \n1790571234\n\n,\n\n  \n\"model\"\n\n:\n \n\"creora-chat\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"choices\"\n\n:\n \n[\n\n    \n{\n\n      \n\"index\"\n\n:\n \n0\n\n,\n\n      \n\"message\"\n\n:\n \n{\n\n        \n\"role\"\n\n:\n \n\"assistant\"\n\n,\n\n        \n\"content\"\n\n:\n \n\"Salt wind on the rocks,\\nthe tide keeps its old promise,\\ngulls write, then erase.\"\n\n      \n}\n\n,\n\n      \n\"logprobs\"\n\n:\n \n\nnull\n\n,\n\n      \n\"finish_reason\"\n\n:\n \n\"stop\"\n\n    \n}\n\n  \n]\n\n,\n\n  \n\"usage\"\n\n:\n \n{\n \n\"prompt_tokens\"\n\n:\n \n131\n\n,\n \n\"completion_tokens\"\n\n:\n \n24\n\n,\n \n\"total_tokens\"\n\n:\n \n155\n \n}\n\n}\n\nAlready using OpenAI?\n The SDKs also read the base URL and the key from the environment, as \nOPENAI_BASE_URL\n and \nOPENAI_API_KEY\n. Set those to Creora's address and your \ncrk_\n key, change the model names to Creora's, and code that builds its client with no arguments talks to Creora. Other tools that speak OpenAI's API and let you change the base URL work the same way, as long as they only send what these pages list.\n\nEndpoints\n\n  \n\nPOST\n\nChat completions\n\nSend a conversation, get the next answer back from \ncreora-chat\n, or from \ncreora-network\n on the GPU network, whole or streamed as it is written.\n\nRead more \n\n  \n\nPOST\n\nImages\n\nOne 1024 by 1024 picture from a prompt, returned as base64 WebP. 16 credits.\n\nRead more \n\n  \n\nPOST\n\nGET\n\nVideos\n\nStart a five second video, check on it, download the MP4 when it is ready.\n\nRead more \n\n  \n\nGET\n\nModels and balance\n\nThe models by name, and how many credits your account holds.\n\nRead more \n\n  \n\nErrors, limits and pricing\n\nEvery status and code, the rate limits, how charging and refunds work, and what each call costs.\n\nRead more \n\n  \n\nCredits and plans\n\nWhat a credit is, the plans and top ups, and paying in stablecoins or Dash.\n\nRead more \n\nMethod\n\nPath\n\nWhat it does\n\nCost\n\n  \n\nPOST\n\n/chat/completions\n\nAn answer in words from \ncreora-chat\n, whole or streamed\n\nBy tokens\n\n  \n\nPOST\n\n/images/generations\n\nOne 1024 by 1024 picture from \ncreora-image\n\n16 credits\n\n  \n\nPOST\n\n/videos\n\nStarts a five second video from \ncreora-video\n\nAbout 256 credits\n\n  \n\nGET\n\n/videos/{id}\n\nA video's status and progress\n\nFree\n\n  \n\nGET\n\n/videos/{id}/content\n\nThe finished MP4\n\nFree\n\n  \n\nGET\n\n/models\n\nThe models you can use now\n\nFree\n\n  \n\nGET\n\n/balance\n\nThe credits your account holds\n\nFree\n\nThat is the whole API. Other parts of OpenAI's, such as embeddings, audio, files, assistants or the Responses API, do not exist here.\n\nConventions\n\n  \n\nRequests\n\nJSON bodies, sent with the header \nContent-Type: application/json\n. A body without it is refused with \n415\n, and cURL's \n-d\n does not set it for you, so the examples always add it. \nPOST /videos\n also takes form fields, which is how the OpenAI SDKs send a video. Bodies are \nUTF-8\n text.\n\n  \n\nResponses\n\nJSON with \nCache-Control: no-store\n, except a streamed answer, which is \ntext/event-stream\n, and a video's content, which is the MP4 itself.\n\n  \n\nTimes\n\nUnix time in whole seconds, in \ncreated\n, \ncreated_at\n and \ncompleted_at\n.\n\n  \n\nIds\n\nA completion is \nchatcmpl-\n followed by 24 hexadecimal characters. A video is \nvideo_\n followed by 24.\n\n  \n\nStreaming\n\nChat answers can arrive as server sent events, in OpenAI's format, ending with \ndata: [DONE]\n. See \nStreaming\n.\n\n  \n\nTimeouts\n\nA whole answer that takes more than about a minute and a half to write can be cut off on its way to you, so stream long answers: a stream sends a keep alive comment every 15 seconds. Videos are made in the background, so no request ever waits for one.\n\n  \n\nBrowsers\n\nThe API sends no CORS headers, so a web page on another site cannot call it. Call it from your server, which is where your key belongs anyway.\n\n  \n\nRequest ids\n\nEvery JSON error answer carries an \nx-request-id\n header. Send one of your own and the same id comes back. See \nthe error object\n.\n\nPaid in credits\n\nEvery call that makes something is paid from your account's credits, the same credits as the rest of Creora. The three free uses a day are for the site, not the API, and a new account has no credits of its own, so add some before your first call: a plan or a top up on \nSettings › Billing\n. What a credit is and how to pay are on \nCredits and API key\n.\n\n  \n\nChat, by the token\n\nCharged when the answer ends, for the tokens in and out. You need a balance above zero to start.\n\n  \n\nPictures and videos, up front\n\nCharged before they are made and given back if they cannot be made. You need the whole price to start.\n\n  \n\nReading is free\n\nModels, your balance, a video's status and its file cost nothing.\n\nAPI calls show in the \nUsage\n table on the billing page with the rest of your use, and anything given back is listed under \nCredits added\n. Every price and rule is on \nErrors, limits and pricing\n.\n\nSafety and privacy\n\nThe API keeps the same two limits as the chat on the site: nothing sexual involving anyone under 18, in any form, including fiction; and nothing sexual about a real, identifiable person.\n\n  \n\nChat\n\nA short system message of Creora's goes before yours. The model talks openly, refuses those two things briefly in its answer, and answers as Creora's model without saying what it runs on.\n\n  \n\nPictures and videos\n\nYour prompt is read before anything is made or charged. One that crosses either line is refused with \n400\n and \ncontent_policy_violation\n, and costs nothing.\n\n  \n\nWhat Creora keeps\n\nNot your chat messages, and not the pictures it sends back: for each call it records the tokens or the price, for your usage. A video keeps its prompt and its MP4 on your account so you can fetch it; both go when your account is deleted.\n\nKeep your key secret\n\nAnyone who has your key can spend your credits. A key opens only the API: it cannot sign in to Creora, open your projects or change your settings. That is still enough to cost you money.\n\n  \nKeep it on your server, in an environment variable or a secrets manager.\n\n  \nNever put it in a web page, an app people download, or a public repository.\n\n  \nMake one key for each place it runs, named after that place, so you can revoke one without touching the others.\n\n  \nIf a key may have leaked, revoke it at once and make a new one.\n\nRevoke a key\n\nOn \nSettings › Developers\n, press \nRevoke\n beside the key and confirm with \nRevoke key\n. It stops working at once and for good: the next request with it gets \n401\n and \ninvalid_api_key\n. There is no undo, so make a new key to replace it. A revoked key no longer counts toward the ten an account can hold, and deleting your account deletes every key it had, with its API videos.",
  "api_chat": "API\n\nChat completions\n\nSend a conversation and get the next message back from Creora's private AI, whole or piece by piece as it is written. The request and the answer have the shape of OpenAI's chat completions, so \nchat.completions.create\n in the OpenAI SDKs works unchanged.\n\nPOST\n\nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1/chat/completions\n\ncreora-chat\n\nRequest body\n\nJSON, sent with \nContent-Type: application/json\n, up to 512 KB.\n\n  \n\nmodel\n\nRequired · string\n\ncreora-chat\n, or \ncreora-network\n to be answered on Creora's GPU network; see \nThe GPU network\n. Any other name is refused with \nmodel_not_found\n.\n\n  \n\nmessages\n\nRequired · array\n\nThe conversation so far, oldest first: 1 to 200 messages, with no more than 60,000 characters of text between them. See \nMessages\n.\n\n  \n\nstream\n\nOptional · boolean\n\nSend the answer as it is written, as server sent events. Default \nfalse\n, which sends it whole once it is finished. See \nStreaming\n.\n\n  \n\nstream_options\n\nOptional · object\n\n{\"include_usage\": true}\n adds one last chunk with the token counts before \n[DONE]\n. Read only when \nstream\n is true.\n\n  \n\nmax_completion_tokens\n\nOptional · integer\n\nThe most tokens the answer may run to, from 1 to 4,096. Default 4,096, which is also the most there is. An answer that reaches it ends with \nfinish_reason\n set to \nlength\n.\n\n  \n\nmax_tokens\n\nOptional · integer\n\nThe older name for the same limit, from 1 to 4,096. When both are sent, \nmax_completion_tokens\n wins.\n\n  \n\ntemperature\n\nOptional · number\n\nFrom 0 to 2. Higher gives more varied answers, lower more focused ones. Left out, the model's own default applies.\n\n  \n\ntop_p\n\nOptional · number\n\nFrom 0 to 1. Only the most likely tokens that together make up this share are considered. Usually change this or \ntemperature\n, not both.\n\n  \n\nstop\n\nOptional · string or array\n\nUp to four sequences, each 1 to 64 characters. Writing stops when the answer reaches one of them, with \nfinish_reason\n \nstop\n.\n\n  \n\npresence_penalty\n\nOptional · number\n\nFrom \n-2\n to \n2\n. Positive values nudge the model toward new topics.\n\n  \n\nfrequency_penalty\n\nOptional · number\n\nFrom \n-2\n to \n2\n. Positive values make it repeat the same words less.\n\n  \n\nn\n\nOptional · integer\n\nOnly \n1\n. One answer is made per request, and any other number is refused.\n\n  \n\nresponse_format\n\nOptional · object\n\nOnly \n{\"type\": \"text\"}\n. JSON mode and JSON schemas are refused.\n\n  \n\nuser\n\nOptional · string\n\nUp to 256 characters. Accepted so code written for OpenAI keeps working, and not used.\n\nA value that is missing, of the wrong type or out of range is refused with \n400\n and \ninvalid_value\n, and \nparam\n names the field, such as \nmax_tokens\n or \nmessages.0.role\n. Parameters this page does not list are ignored, except the ones under \nNot supported\n, which are refused.\n\nMessages\n\nEach message is an object with a \nrole\n and its \ncontent\n.\n\nRole\n\nWhat it is for\n\n  \n\nsystem\n\nInstructions for the whole conversation: the tone, the format, who the model should be for your users. Put it first.\n\n  \n\ndeveloper\n\nTaken as \nsystem\n, for code written against OpenAI's newer name for it.\n\n  \n\nuser\n\nWhat your user said.\n\n  \n\nassistant\n\nWhat the model answered before. Send earlier answers back to carry a conversation on.\n\n  \n\ncontent\n is a string, or an array of text parts such as \n{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"...\"}\n, which are joined with line breaks. \nnull\n counts as empty.\n\n  \nText only. A part of any other type, such as \nimage_url\n or \ninput_audio\n, is refused with \nunsupported_parameter\n, and \nparam\n names the message, such as \nmessages.0.content\n.\n\n  \nThe roles \ntool\n and \nfunction\n are refused, because the API runs no tools.\n\n  \nA message may carry a \nname\n of up to 64 characters. It is accepted and not used.\n\n  \nThe model remembers nothing between requests. To carry a conversation on, send all of it again, with its earlier answers.\n\n  \nAt most 200 messages and 60,000 characters of text across them. More text is refused with \ncontext_length_exceeded\n: drop or summarise the oldest turns.\n\nCreora's system message goes first.\n Before your messages, Creora puts a short system message of its own, about 500 characters: the model answers as Creora's model, does not say what it runs on, and keeps the two limits described in \nSafety and privacy\n. Your own system message follows it and is kept. It is counted in \nprompt_tokens\n, roughly a hundred tokens on every request.\n\nThe response\n\nWithout \nstream\n, one JSON object arrives once the answer is finished:\n\n{\n\n  \n\"id\"\n\n:\n \n\"chatcmpl-9b1e6f0a2c4d8e7f13a5b6c9\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"object\"\n\n:\n \n\"chat.completion\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"created\"\n\n:\n \n1790571234\n\n,\n\n  \n\"model\"\n\n:\n \n\"creora-chat\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"choices\"\n\n:\n \n[\n\n    \n{\n\n      \n\"index\"\n\n:\n \n0\n\n,\n\n      \n\"message\"\n\n:\n \n{\n\n        \n\"role\"\n\n:\n \n\"assistant\"\n\n,\n\n        \n\"content\"\n\n:\n \n\"No. 91 is 7 times 13, so it has divisors other than 1 and itself.\"\n\n      \n}\n\n,\n\n      \n\"logprobs\"\n\n:\n \n\nnull\n\n,\n\n      \n\"finish_reason\"\n\n:\n \n\"stop\"\n\n    \n}\n\n  \n]\n\n,\n\n  \n\"usage\"\n\n:\n \n{\n\n    \n\"prompt_tokens\"\n\n:\n \n196\n\n,\n\n    \n\"completion_tokens\"\n\n:\n \n21\n\n,\n\n    \n\"total_tokens\"\n\n:\n \n217\n\n  \n}\n\n}\n\n  \n\nid\n\nstring\n\nchatcmpl-\n and 24 hexadecimal characters, new for every request.\n\n  \n\nobject\n\nstring\n\nAlways \nchat.completion\n.\n\n  \n\ncreated\n\ninteger\n\nWhen the answer was made, in Unix seconds.\n\n  \n\nmodel\n\nstring\n\nThe model you asked for: \ncreora-chat\n, or \ncreora-network\n.\n\n  \n\nchoices\n\narray\n\nExactly one choice, with \nindex\n \n0\n; the \nmessage\n, whose \nrole\n is \nassistant\n and whose \ncontent\n is the answer; \nlogprobs\n, always \nnull\n; and \nfinish_reason\n.\n\n  \n\nfinish_reason\n\nstring\n\nstop\n when the answer was finished or met a stop sequence; \nlength\n when it ran into the token limit, so it is cut short.\n\n  \n\nusage\n\nobject\n\nprompt_tokens\n (everything sent, Creora's system message included), \ncompletion_tokens\n (the answer) and \ntotal_tokens\n. These are exactly the tokens you are charged for.\n\nThe message never has \ntool_calls\n or \nrefusal\n, and the response has no \nsystem_fingerprint\n. A refusal is an ordinary answer in words.\n\nStreaming\n\nWith \n\"stream\": true\n, the answer arrives as it is written, as server sent events with \nContent-Type: text/event-stream\n. Each event is a line starting \ndata: \n, then one chunk of JSON, then a blank line.\n\n  \nThe first chunk opens the message: its \ndelta\n is \n{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"\"}\n.\n\n  \nThen one chunk for each piece of text, with \ndelta\n \n{\"content\": \"...\"}\n. Join them in order for the whole answer.\n\n  \nA closing chunk has an empty \ndelta\n and the \nfinish_reason\n.\n\n  \nWith \nstream_options.include_usage\n, one more chunk follows, with an empty \nchoices\n array and the \nusage\n.\n\n  \nLast comes \ndata: [DONE]\n.\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"chatcmpl-4c7a0e2f9b1d6e3a8f05c2d7\",\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"created\":1790571234,\"model\":\"creora-chat\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"\"},\"logprobs\":null,\"finish_reason\":null}]}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"chatcmpl-4c7a0e2f9b1d6e3a8f05c2d7\",\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"created\":1790571234,\"model\":\"creora-chat\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"content\":\"Salt wind\"},\"logprobs\":null,\"finish_reason\":null}]}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"chatcmpl-4c7a0e2f9b1d6e3a8f05c2d7\",\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"created\":1790571234,\"model\":\"creora-chat\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"content\":\" on the rocks,\"},\"logprobs\":null,\"finish_reason\":null}]}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"chatcmpl-4c7a0e2f9b1d6e3a8f05c2d7\",\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"created\":1790571234,\"model\":\"creora-chat\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"delta\":{},\"logprobs\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}]}\n\ndata: {\"id\":\"chatcmpl-4c7a0e2f9b1d6e3a8f05c2d7\",\"object\":\"chat.completion.chunk\",\"created\":1790571234,\"model\":\"creora-chat\",\"choices\":[],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":131,\"completion_tokens\":24,\"total_tokens\":155}}\n\ndata: [DONE]\n\nEvery chunk has the same \nid\n, \ncreated\n and \nmodel\n, and its \nobject\n is \nchat.completion.chunk\n. The stream also carries a comment line, \n: keep-alive\n, every 15 seconds, so a quiet connection is not closed on the way. Readers of server sent events, the SDKs among them, skip it.\n\nErrors in a stream.\n Anything that goes wrong before the first word is an ordinary HTTP error with its status, even when you asked for a stream. If something fails part way, the stream sends one event holding an \nerror\n object, in the same shape as \nany other error\n, and ends without \n[DONE]\n. The OpenAI SDKs raise it as an \nAPIError\n. What was written before it is charged.\n\nExamples\n\nA single answer\n\n  \n\ncURL\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\ncurl https://creora.ai/api/v1/chat/completions \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n \\\n  -H \n\"Content-Type: application/json\"\n \\\n  -d \n'{\n    \"model\": \"creora-chat\",\n    \"messages\": [\n      {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You write short, warm product descriptions.\"},\n      {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"A hand thrown stoneware mug, speckled blue glaze.\"}\n    ],\n    \"max_completion_tokens\": 300\n  }'\n\n  \n\nimport\n os\n\nfrom\n openai \nimport\n OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\n\"https://creora.ai/api/v1\"\n, api_key=os.environ[\n\"CREORA_API_KEY\"\n])\n\ncompletion = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\n\"creora-chat\"\n,\n    messages=[\n        {\n\"role\"\n: \n\"system\"\n, \n\"content\"\n: \n\"You write short, warm product descriptions.\"\n},\n        {\n\"role\"\n: \n\"user\"\n, \n\"content\"\n: \n\"A hand thrown stoneware mug, speckled blue glaze.\"\n},\n    ],\n    max_completion_tokens=\n300\n,\n)\n\nprint\n(completion.choices[\n0\n].message.content)\n\nprint\n(completion.usage.total_tokens, \n\"tokens\"\n)\n\n  \n\nimport\n \nOpenAI\n \nfrom\n \n'openai'\n\nconst\n client = \nnew\n \nOpenAI\n({ \nbaseURL\n: \n'https://creora.ai/api/v1'\n, \napiKey\n: process.\nenv\n.\nCREORA_API_KEY\n })\n\nconst\n completion = \nawait\n client.\nchat\n.\ncompletions\n.\ncreate\n({\n  \nmodel\n: \n'creora-chat'\n,\n  \nmessages\n: [\n    { \nrole\n: \n'system'\n, \ncontent\n: \n'You write short, warm product descriptions.'\n },\n    { \nrole\n: \n'user'\n, \ncontent\n: \n'A hand thrown stoneware mug, speckled blue glaze.'\n },\n  ],\n  \nmax_completion_tokens\n: \n300\n,\n})\n\nconsole\n.\nlog\n(completion.\nchoices\n[\n0\n].\nmessage\n.\ncontent\n)\n\nconsole\n.\nlog\n(completion.\nusage\n.\ntotal_tokens\n, \n'tokens'\n)\n\nStreaming\n\n  \n\ncURL\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\n# -N prints each event the moment it arrives\n\ncurl -N https://creora.ai/api/v1/chat/completions \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n \\\n  -H \n\"Content-Type: application/json\"\n \\\n  -d \n'{\n    \"model\": \"creora-chat\",\n    \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Tell me a short story about a lighthouse keeper.\"}],\n    \"stream\": true,\n    \"stream_options\": {\"include_usage\": true}\n  }'\n\n  \n\nimport\n os\n\nfrom\n openai \nimport\n OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\n\"https://creora.ai/api/v1\"\n, api_key=os.environ[\n\"CREORA_API_KEY\"\n])\n\nstream = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\n\"creora-chat\"\n,\n    messages=[{\n\"role\"\n: \n\"user\"\n, \n\"content\"\n: \n\"Tell me a short story about a lighthouse keeper.\"\n}],\n    stream=\nTrue\n,\n    stream_options={\n\"include_usage\"\n: \nTrue\n},\n)\n\nfor\n chunk \nin\n stream:\n    \n# The usage chunk has no choices, so check before reading one.\n\n    \nif\n chunk.choices \nand\n chunk.choices[\n0\n].delta.content:\n        \nprint\n(chunk.choices[\n0\n].delta.content, end=\n\"\"\n, flush=\nTrue\n)\n    \nif\n chunk.usage:\n        \nprint\n(\nf\"\\n\\n\n{chunk.usage.total_tokens}\n tokens\"\n)\n\n  \n\nimport\n \nOpenAI\n \nfrom\n \n'openai'\n\nconst\n client = \nnew\n \nOpenAI\n({ \nbaseURL\n: \n'https://creora.ai/api/v1'\n, \napiKey\n: process.\nenv\n.\nCREORA_API_KEY\n })\n\nconst\n stream = \nawait\n client.\nchat\n.\ncompletions\n.\ncreate\n({\n  \nmodel\n: \n'creora-chat'\n,\n  \nmessages\n: [{ \nrole\n: \n'user'\n, \ncontent\n: \n'Tell me a short story about a lighthouse keeper.'\n }],\n  \nstream\n: \ntrue\n,\n  \nstream_options\n: { \ninclude_usage\n: \ntrue\n },\n})\n\nfor\n \nawait\n (\nconst\n chunk \nof\n stream) {\n  \n// The usage chunk has no choices, so read them with ?.\n\n  process.\nstdout\n.\nwrite\n(chunk.\nchoices\n[\n0\n]?.\ndelta\n?.\ncontent\n ?? \n''\n)\n  \nif\n (chunk.\nusage\n) \nconsole\n.\nlog\n(\n`\\n\\n\n${chunk.usage.total_tokens}\n tokens`\n)\n}\n\nA conversation over several turns\n\nKeep the messages in a list, add each answer to it, and send the whole list every time.\n\n  \n\ncURL\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\ncurl https://creora.ai/api/v1/chat/completions \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n \\\n  -H \n\"Content-Type: application/json\"\n \\\n  -d \n'{\n    \"model\": \"creora-chat\",\n    \"messages\": [\n      {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a patient maths tutor. Answer in two sentences.\"},\n      {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is a prime number?\"},\n      {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"A prime number is a whole number above 1 whose only divisors are 1 and itself. The first few are 2, 3, 5, 7 and 11.\"},\n      {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Is 91 one?\"}\n    ],\n    \"temperature\": 0.3\n  }'\n\n  \n\nimport\n os\n\nfrom\n openai \nimport\n OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\n\"https://creora.ai/api/v1\"\n, api_key=os.environ[\n\"CREORA_API_KEY\"\n])\n\nmessages = [{\n\"role\"\n: \n\"system\"\n, \n\"content\"\n: \n\"You are a patient maths tutor. Answer in two sentences.\"\n}]\n\nfor\n question \nin\n [\n\"What is a prime number?\"\n, \n\"Is 91 one?\"\n]:\n    messages.append({\n\"role\"\n: \n\"user\"\n, \n\"content\"\n: question})\n    completion = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\n\"creora-chat\"\n,\n        messages=messages,\n        temperature=\n0.3\n,\n    )\n    answer = completion.choices[\n0\n].message.content\n    messages.append({\n\"role\"\n: \n\"assistant\"\n, \n\"content\"\n: answer})\n    \nprint\n(\nf\"> \n{question}\n\\n\n{answer}\n\\n\"\n)\n\n  \n\nimport\n \nOpenAI\n \nfrom\n \n'openai'\n\nconst\n client = \nnew\n \nOpenAI\n({ \nbaseURL\n: \n'https://creora.ai/api/v1'\n, \napiKey\n: process.\nenv\n.\nCREORA_API_KEY\n })\n\nconst\n messages = [{ \nrole\n: \n'system'\n, \ncontent\n: \n'You are a patient maths tutor. Answer in two sentences.'\n }]\n\nfor\n (\nconst\n question \nof\n [\n'What is a prime number?'\n, \n'Is 91 one?'\n]) {\n  messages.\npush\n({ \nrole\n: \n'user'\n, \ncontent\n: question })\n  \nconst\n completion = \nawait\n client.\nchat\n.\ncompletions\n.\ncreate\n({\n    \nmodel\n: \n'creora-chat'\n,\n    messages,\n    \ntemperature\n: \n0.3\n,\n  })\n  \nconst\n answer = completion.\nchoices\n[\n0\n].\nmessage\n.\ncontent\n\n  messages.\npush\n({ \nrole\n: \n'assistant'\n, \ncontent\n: answer })\n  \nconsole\n.\nlog\n(\n`> \n${question}\n\\n\n${answer}\n\\n`\n)\n}\n\nWhat it costs\n\n  \n\n260\n\ncredits per million prompt tokens\n\n  \n\n800\n\ncredits per million completion tokens\n\n  \n\n1\n\ncredit, the least a request that writes anything costs\n\nA request costs \n(prompt_tokens × 260 + completion_tokens × 800) ÷ 1,000,000\n credits, rounded up to a whole credit, using the counts in its \nusage\n. A thousand credits is about a dollar, so that is about $0.26 per million tokens in and $0.80 per million out. A short exchange costs one credit. A request near the limits, around 15,000 tokens in (about 60,000 characters of English) and 4,096 out, costs about 8.\n\n  \nYou need a balance above zero to start. Otherwise the request is refused with \n402\n before anything is asked of the model.\n\n  \nThe charge is made when the answer ends. A long answer can take your balance below zero; it is still delivered, and the next request is refused with \n402\n until you add credits.\n\n  \nA whole answer that fails costs nothing, so sending it again never pays twice.\n\n  \nA stream is charged for what was written, whether it finishes, fails part way or you close it. The same goes for a whole answer you stop waiting for.\n\n  \nWhen the token count does not arrive, as when a stream is cut short, it is reckoned from the text instead: one token for every three bytes.\n\nWorked examples and the prices of every endpoint are on \nErrors, limits and pricing\n.\n\nThe GPU network\n\nPOST\n\nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1/chat/completions\n\ncreora-network\n\nSend \n\"model\": \"creora-network\"\n to be answered by an open model on Creora's \nGPU network\n: machines run by providers, each sending its proof to Creora every 10 minutes, so that whoever runs the machine never sees what you ask. Your request is sealed on Creora's server to a key born inside the machine, and the answer is signed there and checked before it reaches you.\n\n  \nEverything above holds for it: the same body, messages, limits and refusals, the same response and the same stream, with \nmodel\n set to \ncreora-network\n. Creora's short system message goes first here too.\n\n  \nIt is charged for the tokens in and out, at its own price per million tokens, shown on the \nGPU network page\n. Before a machine is asked, the most the request could cost is set aside from your balance: every byte of the messages counted as a prompt token, and all of \nmax_tokens\n (4,096 when you send none) as completion tokens. When the answer ends you are charged what it used and the rest is given back at once; only what was used shows in your usage. Without that much in hand it is refused with \n402\n and the amount, so a smaller \nmax_tokens\n sets aside less.\n\n  \nIf you stop reading part way, whole or streamed, you are charged for what was written before you went, as with \ncreora-chat\n. Once a machine has taken the request, it has read your messages, so if you go before any text is written you are charged for those as prompt tokens, reckoned from their length, and for no completion tokens. An account can have four of these requests running at once; a fifth is refused with \n429\n and \ntoo_many_requests\n.\n\n  \nIt is answered only while at least one machine's proof holds, and \nthe model list\n names it only then. When no machine is serving it, the request is refused with \n503\n and \nno_capacity\n, and nothing is charged: \nNo network node is serving this model right now. Try again soon, or use \ncreora-chat\n.\n\n  \nAn answer whose signature does not check out is never passed on. The request is refused with \n502\n and \nupstream_error\n, and nothing is charged for it.\n\ncurl https://creora.ai/api/v1/chat/completions \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n \\\n  -H \n\"Content-Type: application/json\"\n \\\n  -d \n'{\n    \"model\": \"creora-network\",\n    \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise this contract in three sentences.\"}]\n  }'\n\nNot supported\n\nThese OpenAI parameters are refused with \n400\n and \nunsupported_parameter\n, with \nparam\n naming the parameter, whenever they are sent with any value other than \nnull\n or \nfalse\n. An empty \ntools\n array is refused too, so leave the field out.\n\ntools\n, \ntool_choice\n, \nfunctions\n, \nfunction_call\n, \nparallel_tool_calls\n, \nlogprobs\n, \ntop_logprobs\n, \nseed\n, \naudio\n, \nmodalities\n, \nprediction\n\nAlso refused: \nn\n other than 1, a \nresponse_format\n other than text, pictures, audio or files in messages, and the roles \ntool\n and \nfunction\n. Other parameters OpenAI takes, such as \nmetadata\n, \nstore\n, \nlogit_bias\n or \nservice_tier\n, are accepted and have no effect.\n\nWords only.\n Unlike the chat on the site, this endpoint only writes text: asking it for a picture or a video gets words back. Use \nImages\n and \nVideos\n for those.",
  "api_errors": "API\n\nErrors, limits and pricing\n\nWhat every error looks like and what to do about it, the limits on each key and each account, and exactly how calls are charged, refunded and priced.\n\nThe error object\n\nEvery error is JSON in the shape OpenAI uses, so the OpenAI SDKs raise their usual exceptions:\n\n{\n\n  \n\"error\"\n\n:\n \n{\n\n    \n\"message\"\n\n:\n \n\"That costs 16 credits and this account has 10.\"\n\n,\n\n    \n\"type\"\n\n:\n \n\"insufficient_credits\"\n\n,\n\n    \n\"code\"\n\n:\n \n\"insufficient_credits\"\n\n,\n\n    \n\"param\"\n\n:\n \n\nnull\n\n  \n}\n\n}\n\n  \n\nmessage\n\nstring\n\nA sentence for a person to read, in Creora's words. Show it or log it, but do not match on it: the wording can change.\n\n  \n\ntype\n\nstring\n\nThe broad kind: \ninvalid_request_error\n, \nauthentication_error\n, \ninsufficient_credits\n, \nnot_found_error\n, \nrate_limit_error\n or \napi_error\n.\n\n  \n\ncode\n\nstring\n\nThe exact reason, from the table below. This is the field to branch on.\n\n  \n\nparam\n\nstring or null\n\nThe field at fault, such as \nsize\n, \nprompt\n or \nmessages.0.content\n, or \nnull\n when no one field is.\n\nError answers carry three headers worth knowing:\n\n  \n\nx-request-id\n\nAn id for the request. Send your own \nx-request-id\n (letters, digits, dots, underscores and hyphens, up to 128 characters) and the same one comes back; otherwise Creora makes one. The OpenAI SDKs put it on the exception, as \nrequest_id\n in Python and \nrequestID\n in JavaScript. Quote it if you ask for help.\n\n  \n\nRetry-After\n\nOn a \n429\n: how many whole seconds to wait before sending again.\n\n  \n\nCache-Control\n\nAlways \nno-store\n.\n\nA failure part way through a streamed answer arrives inside the stream instead, as one \ndata:\n event holding the same \nerror\n object, and the stream ends without \n[DONE]\n. See \nStreaming\n.\n\nStatus codes\n\nStatus\n\nCode\n\nWhat happened\n\nWhat to do\n\n  \n\n400\n\ninvalid_json\n\nThe body is not valid JSON.\n\nFix the body.\n\n  \n\n400\n\ninvalid_form\n\nA video's form fields could not be read.\n\nSend JSON, or a well formed \nmultipart/form-data\n body.\n\n  \n\n400\n\ninvalid_value\n\nA field is missing, of the wrong type or out of range, such as \nmax_tokens\n above 4,096 or a message with the role \ntool\n. \nparam\n names it.\n\nFix that field.\n\n  \n\n400\n\nunsupported_parameter\n\nSomething this API does not serve: \ntools\n, \nn\n above 1, a picture in a message, a JSON \nresponse_format\n, another size or length, a file in a video's form. \nparam\n names it.\n\nLeave it out.\n\n  \n\n400\n\nmodel_not_found\n\nA model other than the endpoint's own.\n\nUse \ncreora-chat\n, \ncreora-image\n or \ncreora-video\n, or \ncreora-network\n for chat where the GPU network is offered.\n\n  \n\n400\n\ncontext_length_exceeded\n\nThe messages hold more than 60,000 characters of text.\n\nDrop or summarise the oldest turns.\n\n  \n\n400\n\ncontent_policy_violation\n\nA picture or video that may not be made, or one that was flagged as it was made. Nothing is charged.\n\nAsk for something else.\n\n  \n\n400\n\ninvalid_request\n\nThe body is not \nUTF-8\n text, or the request could not be served as it was sent.\n\nCheck the encoding, then change the request.\n\n  \n\n401\n\nmissing_api_key\n\nNo \nAuthorization: Bearer\n header.\n\nSend your key.\n\n  \n\n401\n\ninvalid_api_key\n\nThe key is mistyped, malformed or revoked.\n\nCheck it, or make a new one in \nSettings › Developers\n.\n\n  \n\n402\n\ninsufficient_credits\n\nNo credits left for a chat request, or fewer than the price of a picture or a video, or than the most a \ncreora-network\n request could cost. The message gives the price and your balance.\n\nAdd credits on \nSettings › Billing\n, or ask a network request for a smaller \nmax_tokens\n.\n\n  \n\n404\n\nnot_found\n\nNo such video on this key's account, or a video whose file is no longer stored.\n\nCheck the id, and that the key belongs to the account that made it.\n\n  \n\n404\n\nvideo_not_ready\n\nA video's content was asked for before it was completed.\n\nWait until its status is \ncompleted\n.\n\n  \n\n413\n\nrequest_too_large\n\nThe body is over the endpoint's cap.\n\nSend less. See \nRequest limits\n.\n\n  \n\n415\n\nunsupported_media_type\n\nThe body was not sent as JSON (or, for a video, as form fields).\n\nAdd \nContent-Type: application/json\n.\n\n  \n\n429\n\nrate_limit_exceeded\n\nToo many requests of that kind on this key.\n\nWait for \nRetry-After\n, then send it again.\n\n  \n\n429\n\ntoo_many_videos\n\nThree of the account's videos are already being made.\n\nWait for one to finish; \nRetry-After\n says 30.\n\n  \n\n429\n\ntoo_many_requests\n\nFour of the account's \ncreora-network\n requests are already running.\n\nWait for one to finish; \nRetry-After\n says 5.\n\n  \n\n500\n\ninternal_error\n\nSomething went wrong on Creora's side.\n\nSend it again shortly.\n\n  \n\n502\n\nupstream_error\n\nThe model failed to answer.\n\nSend it again.\n\n  \n\n503\n\nservice_unavailable\n\nCreora is busy for a moment, or briefly unavailable.\n\nWait a little, then send it again.\n\n  \n\n503\n\nno_capacity\n\nA request for \ncreora-network\n while no machine of the GPU network is serving it. The message is \nNo network node is serving this model right now. Try again soon, or use \ncreora-chat\n.\n Nothing is charged.\n\nTry again soon, or send it to \ncreora-chat\n.\n\nTreat any \n5xx\n the same way, whatever its code. A few answers carry no JSON body: \n416\n for a range past the end of a video, \n405\n for a method a path does not take, and \n404\n for a path that does not exist at all.\n\nThe order of checks\n\nEvery request passes the same gates, in this order, and a request refused at any of them costs nothing.\n\n  \n\n401\n\nKey\n\n  \n\n  \n\n429\n\nRate limit\n\n  \n\n  \n\n400 413 415\n\nBody\n\n  \n\n  \n\n402\n\nCredits\n\n  \n\n  \n\n400\n\nPrompt check\n\n  \n\n  \n\n200\n\nMade\n\nThe prompt check is for pictures and videos only. A video also checks its place among your three before its price is taken. A request for \ncreora-network\n checks that a machine of the network is serving it, and answers \n503\n \nno_capacity\n when none is, before it checks your credits; it then sets aside the most it could cost, or is refused with \n402\n when your balance does not hold that, or with \n429\n \ntoo_many_requests\n when four of your network requests are already running. A request that gets past the rate limit counts toward it, even when a later gate refuses it.\n\nHandling errors in code\n\nThe OpenAI SDKs turn each status into an exception of its own:\n\nStatus\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\n400\n\nopenai.BadRequestError\n\nOpenAI.BadRequestError\n\n  \n\n401\n\nopenai.AuthenticationError\n\nOpenAI.AuthenticationError\n\n  \n\n404\n\nopenai.NotFoundError\n\nOpenAI.NotFoundError\n\n  \n\n429\n\nopenai.RateLimitError\n\nOpenAI.RateLimitError\n\n  \n\n500\n, \n502\n, \n503\n\nopenai.InternalServerError\n\nOpenAI.InternalServerError\n\n  \n\n402\n, \n413\n, \n415\n\nopenai.APIStatusError\n\nOpenAI.APIError\n\n  \n\nError inside a stream\n\nopenai.APIError\n\nOpenAI.APIError\n\nEach carries the status, the \ncode\n and the \nparam\n. Catch the specific ones first:\n\n  \n\ncURL\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\n# -i prints the status line and headers (x-request-id, Retry-After) before the body\n\ncurl -i https://creora.ai/api/v1/images/generations \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n \\\n  -H \n\"Content-Type: application/json\"\n \\\n  -H \n\"x-request-id: my-job-42\"\n \\\n  -d \n'{\"model\": \"creora-image\", \"prompt\": \"A lighthouse in a storm\", \"size\": \"512x512\"}'\n\n# HTTP/2 400\n\n# x-request-id: my-job-42\n\n#\n\n# {\"error\":{\"message\":\"Pictures are 1024x1024.\",\"type\":\"invalid_request_error\",\"code\":\"unsupported_parameter\",\"param\":\"size\"}}\n\n  \n\nimport\n os\n\nimport\n openai\n\nfrom\n openai \nimport\n OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(\n    base_url=\n\"https://creora.ai/api/v1\"\n,\n    api_key=os.environ[\n\"CREORA_API_KEY\"\n],\n    max_retries=\n2\n,  \n# the default: 429 and 5xx answers are retried for you\n\n)\n\ntry\n:\n    result = client.images.generate(model=\n\"creora-image\"\n, prompt=\n\"A lighthouse in a storm\"\n)\n\nexcept\n openai.BadRequestError \nas\n e:\n    \n# Fix the request: e.code says what is wrong, e.param which field\n\n    \nprint\n(\n\"Refused:\"\n, e.code, e.param)\n\nexcept\n openai.AuthenticationError:\n    \nprint\n(\n\"Check CREORA_API_KEY: missing, mistyped or revoked\"\n)\n\nexcept\n openai.RateLimitError:\n    \nprint\n(\n\"Still limited after the retries: slow down\"\n)\n\nexcept\n openai.APIStatusError \nas\n e:\n    \n# 402 insufficient_credits, 404, 413, 415, and 5xx once the retries are spent\n\n    \nprint\n(e.status_code, e.code, e.request_id)\n\n  \n\nimport\n \nOpenAI\n \nfrom\n \n'openai'\n\nconst\n client = \nnew\n \nOpenAI\n({\n  \nbaseURL\n: \n'https://creora.ai/api/v1'\n,\n  \napiKey\n: process.\nenv\n.\nCREORA_API_KEY\n,\n  \nmaxRetries\n: \n2\n, \n// the default: 429 and 5xx answers are retried for you\n\n})\n\ntry\n {\n  \nawait\n client.\nimages\n.\ngenerate\n({ \nmodel\n: \n'creora-image'\n, \nprompt\n: \n'A lighthouse in a storm'\n })\n} \ncatch\n (err) {\n  \nif\n (err \ninstanceof\n \nOpenAI\n.\nBadRequestError\n) {\n    \n// Fix the request: err.code says what is wrong, err.param which field\n\n    \nconsole\n.\nlog\n(\n'Refused:'\n, err.\ncode\n, err.\nparam\n)\n  } \nelse\n \nif\n (err \ninstanceof\n \nOpenAI\n.\nAuthenticationError\n) {\n    \nconsole\n.\nlog\n(\n'Check CREORA_API_KEY: missing, mistyped or revoked'\n)\n  } \nelse\n \nif\n (err \ninstanceof\n \nOpenAI\n.\nRateLimitError\n) {\n    \nconsole\n.\nlog\n(\n'Still limited after the retries: slow down'\n)\n  } \nelse\n \nif\n (err \ninstanceof\n \nOpenAI\n.\nAPIError\n) {\n    \n// 402 insufficient_credits, 404, 413, 415, and 5xx once the retries are spent\n\n    \nconsole\n.\nlog\n(err.\nstatus\n, err.\ncode\n, err.\nrequestID\n)\n  } \nelse\n {\n    \nthrow\n err\n  }\n}\n\nRate limits\n\nRequests\n\nLimit\n\nCounted for\n\n  \n\nPOST /chat/completions\n\n60 a minute\n\nEach key\n\n  \n\nPOST /images/generations\n\n20 a minute\n\nEach key\n\n  \n\nPOST /videos\n\n30 an hour\n\nEach key\n\n  \n\nEvery \nGET\n: models, balance, a video, its content\n\n120 a minute, shared\n\nEach key\n\n  \n\nVideos queued or being made\n\n3 at once\n\nEach account\n\n  \n\ncreora-network\n requests running\n\n4 at once\n\nEach account\n\n  \n\nLive keys\n\n10\n\nEach account\n\n  \nThe windows slide: each request counts for exactly one minute (or, for videos, one hour) from when it came in. \nRetry-After\n is the number of seconds until the oldest one drops out.\n\n  \nEach key has its own counts. A request with a missing or invalid key counts against nothing.\n\n  \nPast a limit the answer is \n429\n \nrate_limit_exceeded\n. A fourth video while three are being made is \n429\n \ntoo_many_videos\n, with \nRetry-After: 30\n. A fifth \ncreora-network\n request while four are running is \n429\n \ntoo_many_requests\n, with \nRetry-After: 5\n.\n\nRequest limits\n\nEndpoint\n\nBody\n\nInside it\n\n  \n\n/chat/completions\n\n512 KB\n\n1 to 200 messages; 60,000 characters of text across them; an answer of up to 4,096 tokens; up to 4 stop sequences of 1 to 64 characters\n\n  \n\n/images/generations\n\n16 KB\n\nA prompt of 1 to 1,500 characters; one picture, 1024 by 1024\n\n  \n\n/videos\n\n16 KB, JSON or form\n\nA prompt of 1 to 1,500 characters; five seconds, 1280 by 720\n\nA body over its cap is \n413\n \nrequest_too_large\n; anything else out of bounds is \n400\n with \nparam\n naming it.\n\nHow charging works\n\nThe API is paid in credits only: the three free uses a day are for the site, and a new account has no credits until you add some. A credit is a thousandth of a dollar.\n\n  \n\nChat is charged after\n\nA \ncreora-chat\n request is let in when your balance is above zero, and charged for its tokens when the answer ends. A long answer can take the balance below zero; it is still delivered, and later requests get \n402\n until you add credits. A whole answer that fails costs nothing. A stream is charged for what was written, whether it finishes, breaks or you close it, and so is a whole answer you stop waiting for.\n\n  \n\nThe GPU network sets aside first\n\nA \ncreora-network\n request needs the most it could cost in hand: every byte of its messages counted as a prompt token, and all of its \nmax_tokens\n as completion tokens. That is set aside in one step before a machine is asked, so requests sent together can never spend more than you have, and when the answer ends you keep all of it but what the tokens used. A stream, or a whole answer you stop waiting for, is charged for what was written; an answer that fails or does not verify costs nothing.\n\n  \n\nPictures are charged before\n\nYou need 16 credits in hand. They are checked and taken in one step before the picture is made, so requests sent together can never spend more than you have. If no picture comes back, they are given back, once.\n\n  \n\nVideos are charged as they start\n\nYou need a balance above zero, then the video's whole quote, which is taken when it is queued. If it cannot be started, fails, is refused while being made or runs past 15 minutes, the credits are given back, once.\n\n  \n\nRefusals are free\n\nA request turned away by a check, including a prompt that may not be made, costs nothing. Reading a prompt before a picture or video is made is never charged.\n\nWith no credits, a chat or video request is refused with \nThis account has no credits left.\n A picture, a video when you have some credits but fewer than its quote, or a \ncreora-network\n request when you have fewer than the most it could cost, is refused with its price and your balance, such as \nThat costs 16 credits and this account has 10.\n Your spending shows day by day in the \nUsage\n table on \nSettings › Billing\n, beside the rest of your use, and every refund is listed under \nCredits added\n. \nGET /balance\n gives the balance itself; see \nYour balance\n.\n\nPricing\n\nEndpoint\n\nModel\n\nPrice in credits\n\nIn dollars\n\n  \n\nPOST /chat/completions\n\ncreora-chat\n\n260 per million prompt tokens and 800 per million completion tokens, rounded up to a whole credit per request\n\n$0.26 and $0.80 per million\n\n  \n\nPOST /images/generations\n\ncreora-image\n\n16 a picture\n\n$0.016\n\n  \n\nPOST /videos\n\ncreora-video\n\nIts quote when it starts, about 256 for five seconds\n\nAbout $0.26\n\n  \n\nPOST /chat/completions\n\ncreora-network\n\nIts own price per million prompt and completion tokens, shown on the \nGPU network page\n, rounded up to a whole credit per request\n\nA thousand credits to the dollar\n\n  \n\nEvery \nGET\n\nFree\n\nFree\n\nA chat request costs \n(prompt_tokens × 260 + completion_tokens × 800) ÷ 1,000,000\n, rounded up, using the counts in the answer's \nusage\n. \nprompt_tokens\n includes Creora's short system message, roughly a hundred tokens. When the count does not arrive, as when a stream is cut short, it is reckoned from the text at one token for every three bytes.\n\nPrompt tokens\n\nCompletion tokens\n\nExact cost\n\nCharged\n\n  \n\n150\n\n50\n\n0.12\n\n1 credit\n\n  \n\n2,000\n\n800\n\n1.79\n\n2 credits\n\n  \n\n10,000\n\n2,000\n\n6.08\n\n7 credits\n\n  \n\n15,000\n\n4,096\n\n10.70\n\n11 credits\n\nFor scale, the 12,000 credits of the Starter plan pay for 750 pictures, about 46 videos, or thousands of short chat answers. Plans, top ups and paying are on \nCredits and API key\n.\n\nRetries and idempotency\n\nAnswer\n\nSend it again?\n\n  \n\n429\n\nYes, after \nRetry-After\n seconds.\n\n  \n\n500\n, \n502\n, \n503\n\nYes, after a short wait, waiting longer each time.\n\n  \n\nA stream that breaks part way\n\nYes, but it starts over, and what was already written is charged.\n\n  \n\n400\n, \n401\n, \n402\n, \n404\n, \n413\n, \n415\n\nNot as it is: fix the request, the key or the balance first.\n\n  \n\nFailures cost nothing.\n A whole chat answer that fails is not charged, a picture that is not made is refunded, and a video that does not start is refunded, so sending a failed request again never pays twice.\n\n  \n\nThe SDKs retry for you.\n By default the OpenAI SDKs retry \n429\n and \n5xx\n answers and dropped connections twice, waiting longer each time and following \nRetry-After\n. Change it with \nmax_retries\n in Python or \nmaxRetries\n in JavaScript. With your own client, wait \nRetry-After\n when it is there; otherwise start at a second and double the wait each time, with a little randomness, for a handful of tries.\n\n  \n\nThere are no idempotency keys.\n An \nIdempotency-Key\n header is ignored. A request that finished on Creora's side is charged even if its answer never reached you, for instance when your connection dropped, and sending it again makes and charges it again.\n\n  \n\nGive requests time.\n The SDKs wait up to ten minutes by default, which is enough for anything here. Do not cut a whole chat answer short with a tight timeout: stream long answers instead.\n\n  \n\nKeep video ids.\n The answer to \nPOST /videos\n is the only place a video's id appears. If you lose it, the video is still made and charged, but you cannot fetch it.",
  "api_images": "API\n\nImages\n\nDescribe a picture and get it back: one 1024 by 1024 WebP a request, returned inside the JSON as base64. It is the same call as OpenAI's image generation, so \nimages.generate\n in the OpenAI SDKs works unchanged.\n\nPOST\n\nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1/images/generations\n\ncreora-image\n\n  \n\n16\n\ncredits a picture, given back if none comes\n\n  \n\n1024\n\npixels square, as WebP\n\n  \n\n1\n\npicture a request\n\n  \n\n20\n\nrequests a minute for each key\n\nRequest body\n\nJSON, sent with \nContent-Type: application/json\n, up to 16 KB.\n\n  \n\nprompt\n\nRequired · string\n\nWhat to make, 1 to 1,500 characters once spaces at either end are trimmed. The picture is made from your words as you wrote them; Creora does not rewrite them. Name the subject, the setting, the style and the light.\n\n  \n\nmodel\n\nOptional · string\n\ncreora-image\n, which is also the default. Any other name is refused with \nmodel_not_found\n.\n\n  \n\nn\n\nOptional · integer\n\nOnly \n1\n. For several pictures, send several requests.\n\n  \n\nsize\n\nOptional · string\n\nOnly \n1024x1024\n, the default. Any other size is refused.\n\n  \n\nresponse_format\n\nOptional · string\n\nOnly \nb64_json\n, the default. \nurl\n is refused: pictures are returned, not hosted.\n\n  \n\nuser\n\nOptional · string\n\nUp to 256 characters. Accepted so code written for OpenAI keeps working, and not used.\n\nOther parameters OpenAI's image models take, such as \nquality\n, \nstyle\n, \nbackground\n or \noutput_format\n, are accepted and have no effect: every picture is a 1024 by 1024 WebP. A value Creora does not serve is refused with \n400\n and \nunsupported_parameter\n, and an empty or missing \nprompt\n with \ninvalid_value\n; \nparam\n names the field either way.\n\nThe response\n\n{\n\n  \n\"created\"\n\n:\n \n1790571234\n\n,\n\n  \n\"data\"\n\n:\n \n[\n\n    \n{\n \n\"b64_json\"\n\n:\n \n\"UklGRqj3AQBXRUJQVlA4IJz3AQ...\"\n \n}\n\n  \n]\n\n,\n\n  \n\"output_format\"\n\n:\n \n\"webp\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"size\"\n\n:\n \n\"1024x1024\"\n\n}\n\n  \n\ncreated\n\ninteger\n\nWhen the picture was made, in Unix seconds.\n\n  \n\ndata\n\narray\n\nExactly one item, whose \nb64_json\n is the picture's bytes in base64. The string above is shortened; a real one is long. Decoded, the bytes start with \nRIFF\n and are a WebP file.\n\n  \n\noutput_format\n\nstring\n\nAlways \nwebp\n. Save the file with a \n.webp\n name; every current browser opens it, and so do most image tools.\n\n  \n\nsize\n\nstring\n\nAlways \n1024x1024\n.\n\nExamples\n\nMake a picture and save it as a file:\n\n  \n\ncURL\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\n# jq pulls the base64 out of the JSON, base64 turns it back into bytes\n\ncurl -s https://creora.ai/api/v1/images/generations \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n \\\n  -H \n\"Content-Type: application/json\"\n \\\n  -d \n'{\"model\": \"creora-image\", \"prompt\": \"A lighthouse in a storm at dusk, oil painting, heavy brush strokes\"}'\n \\\n  | jq -r \n'.data[0].b64_json'\n | \nbase64\n --decode > lighthouse.webp\n\n  \n\nimport\n base64\n\nimport\n os\n\nfrom\n openai \nimport\n OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\n\"https://creora.ai/api/v1\"\n, api_key=os.environ[\n\"CREORA_API_KEY\"\n])\n\nresult = client.images.generate(\n    model=\n\"creora-image\"\n,\n    prompt=\n\"A lighthouse in a storm at dusk, oil painting, heavy brush strokes\"\n,\n)\n\nwith\n \nopen\n(\n\"lighthouse.webp\"\n, \n\"wb\"\n) \nas\n file:\n    file.write(base64.b64decode(result.data[\n0\n].b64_json))\n\n  \n\nimport\n { writeFile } \nfrom\n \n'node:fs/promises'\n\nimport\n \nOpenAI\n \nfrom\n \n'openai'\n\nconst\n client = \nnew\n \nOpenAI\n({ \nbaseURL\n: \n'https://creora.ai/api/v1'\n, \napiKey\n: process.\nenv\n.\nCREORA_API_KEY\n })\n\nconst\n result = \nawait\n client.\nimages\n.\ngenerate\n({\n  \nmodel\n: \n'creora-image'\n,\n  \nprompt\n: \n'A lighthouse in a storm at dusk, oil painting, heavy brush strokes'\n,\n})\n\nawait\n \nwriteFile\n(\n'lighthouse.webp'\n, \nBuffer\n.\nfrom\n(result.\ndata\n[\n0\n].\nb64_json\n, \n'base64'\n))\n\nTo show the picture in a web page without saving it, put the base64 in a data URL: \ndata:image/webp;base64,\n followed by \nb64_json\n.\n\nPrice and refunds\n\n  \nA picture costs \n16 credits\n, about $0.016.\n\n  \nYou need at least 16 credits to ask. With fewer, the request is refused with \n402\n and a message such as \nThat costs 16 credits and this account has 10.\n Nothing is made.\n\n  \nThe price is taken before the picture is made. Several requests at once therefore never spend more than you have: each one either gets its 16 credits or a \n402\n.\n\n  \nIf no picture comes back, for whatever reason, the 16 credits are given back, once. The return is listed under \nCredits added\n on the billing page as \nReturned for an API picture that could not be made\n.\n\n  \nA prompt that is refused costs nothing, and reading your prompt before the picture is made is never charged.\n\nRefusals\n\nTwo lines hold here as on the site: nothing sexual involving anyone under 18, and no sexual images of a real person. Your prompt is read for both before anything is made or charged.\n\nWhen\n\nAnswer\n\nMessage\n\nCharged\n\n  \n\nThe prompt is sexual and involves someone under 18\n\n400\n \ncontent_policy_violation\n, \nparam\n \nprompt\n\nThat cannot be made. Nothing sexual involving anyone under 18, ever.\n\nNothing\n\n  \n\nThe prompt is sexual and about a real person\n\n400\n \ncontent_policy_violation\n, \nparam\n \nprompt\n\nThat cannot be made. No sexual images of a real person.\n\nNothing\n\n  \n\nThe finished picture is flagged as it is made\n\n400\n \ncontent_policy_violation\n\nThat could not be made. Try describing it differently.\n\nGiven back\n\nThese are not errors to retry: change what you ask for, and send that instead.\n\nLimits\n\n  \n\nRequests for each key\n\n20 a minute\n\n  \n\nPictures a request\n\n1\n\n  \n\nSize\n\n1024 by 1024, WebP\n\n  \n\nPrompt\n\n1 to 1,500 characters\n\n  \n\nRequest body\n\n16 KB\n\n  \n\nPrice\n\n16 credits\n\nPast the rate limit the answer is \n429\n with a \nRetry-After\n header. Every limit and error is on \nErrors, limits and pricing\n.",
  "api_models": "API\n\nModels and balance\n\nTwo small reads: the models the API offers, by Creora's own names, and how many credits your account holds. Both are free.\n\nList models\n\nGET\n\nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1/models\n\nAnswers the models you can use right now, in OpenAI's list shape, so \nmodels.list\n in the OpenAI SDKs works unchanged. The list is the same for every account. It always holds the three below. \ncreora-network\n, the model of Creora's \nGPU network\n, is added only while a machine of the network is serving it, so it can come and go from one call to the next.\n\n{\n\n  \n\"object\"\n\n:\n \n\"list\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"data\"\n\n:\n \n[\n\n    \n{\n \n\"id\"\n\n:\n \n\"creora-chat\"\n\n,\n \n\"object\"\n\n:\n \n\"model\"\n\n,\n \n\"created\"\n\n:\n \n1790467200\n\n,\n \n\"owned_by\"\n\n:\n \n\"creora\"\n \n}\n\n,\n\n    \n{\n \n\"id\"\n\n:\n \n\"creora-image\"\n\n,\n \n\"object\"\n\n:\n \n\"model\"\n\n,\n \n\"created\"\n\n:\n \n1790467200\n\n,\n \n\"owned_by\"\n\n:\n \n\"creora\"\n \n}\n\n,\n\n    \n{\n \n\"id\"\n\n:\n \n\"creora-video\"\n\n,\n \n\"object\"\n\n:\n \n\"model\"\n\n,\n \n\"created\"\n\n:\n \n1790467200\n\n,\n \n\"owned_by\"\n\n:\n \n\"creora\"\n \n}\n\n  \n]\n\n}\n\n  \n\nid\n\nstring\n\nThe name to send as \nmodel\n.\n\n  \n\nobject\n\nstring\n\nAlways \nmodel\n.\n\n  \n\ncreated\n\ninteger\n\nWhen the API opened, 27 September 2026, in Unix seconds.\n\n  \n\nowned_by\n\nstring\n\nAlways \ncreora\n.\n\n  \n\ncURL\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\ncurl https://creora.ai/api/v1/models \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n\n  \n\nimport\n os\n\nfrom\n openai \nimport\n OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\n\"https://creora.ai/api/v1\"\n, api_key=os.environ[\n\"CREORA_API_KEY\"\n])\n\nfor\n model \nin\n client.models.\nlist\n():\n    \nprint\n(model.\nid\n)\n\n  \n\nimport\n \nOpenAI\n \nfrom\n \n'openai'\n\nconst\n client = \nnew\n \nOpenAI\n({ \nbaseURL\n: \n'https://creora.ai/api/v1'\n, \napiKey\n: process.\nenv\n.\nCREORA_API_KEY\n })\n\nfor\n \nawait\n (\nconst\n model \nof\n client.\nmodels\n.\nlist\n()) {\n  \nconsole\n.\nlog\n(model.\nid\n)\n}\n\nAsking for one model by name, as \nmodels.retrieve\n does with \nGET /models/{id}\n, is not supported and answers \n404\n. Everything there is to know about each model is in the table below.\n\nThe models\n\nModel\n\nEndpoint\n\nWhat it makes\n\nPrice\n\n  \n\ncreora-chat\n\n/chat/completions\n\nAnswers in words, up to 4,096 tokens each, whole or streamed\n\n260 credits per million tokens in, 800 per million out\n\n  \n\ncreora-image\n\n/images/generations\n\nOne 1024 by 1024 WebP picture a request\n\n16 credits a picture\n\n  \n\ncreora-video\n\n/videos\n\nFive second, 1280 by 720 MP4 clips\n\nIts quote, about 256 credits\n\n  \n\ncreora-network\n\n/chat/completions\n\nAnswers in words from an open model on Creora's GPU network, sealed end to end, whole or streamed\n\nIts own price per million tokens in and out, shown on the \nGPU network page\n\nEach endpoint takes only its own models: chat takes \ncreora-chat\n or \ncreora-network\n, pictures and videos their one each. Sending another name, such as an OpenAI model's, is refused with \n400\n and \nmodel_not_found\n, and the message names the model to use. On the image and video endpoints \nmodel\n may be left out; on chat it is required.\n\nThe network serves as machines join.\n \ncreora-network\n is answered only while at least one machine's proof holds. When none is serving, a request for it is refused with \n503\n and \nno_capacity\n, nothing is charged, and \ncreora-chat\n is there instead. On a server where the network is not offered at all, the name is refused with \nmodel_not_found\n. See \nStatus codes\n.\n\nYour balance\n\nGET\n\nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1/balance\n\nAnswers the credits the key's account holds right now. This is Creora's own addition, not part of OpenAI's API, so the SDKs have no method for it: call it over plain HTTP.\n\n{\n\n  \n\"object\"\n\n:\n \n\"balance\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"credits\"\n\n:\n \n12000\n\n}\n\n  \n\nobject\n\nstring\n\nAlways \nbalance\n.\n\n  \n\ncredits\n\ninteger\n\nWhole credits. A thousand is about a dollar. It can be below zero after a chat answer that ran past your last credits; the next credits you add settle it.\n\n  \n\ncURL\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\ncurl https://creora.ai/api/v1/balance \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n\n  \n\nimport\n os\n\nimport\n httpx  \n# installed with the openai package\n\nresponse = httpx.get(\n    \n\"https://creora.ai/api/v1/balance\"\n,\n    headers={\n\"Authorization\"\n: \nf\"Bearer \n{os.environ[\n'CREORA_API_KEY'\n]}\n\"\n},\n)\nresponse.raise_for_status()\n\nprint\n(response.json()[\n\"credits\"\n], \n\"credits\"\n)\n\n  \n\n// fetch is built into Node 18 and later\n\nconst\n response = \nawait\n \nfetch\n(\n'https://creora.ai/api/v1/balance'\n, {\n  \nheaders\n: { \nAuthorization\n: \n`Bearer \n${process.env.CREORA_API_KEY}\n`\n },\n})\n\nif\n (!response.\nok\n) \nthrow\n \nnew\n \nError\n(\n`Balance check failed: \n${response.status}\n`\n)\n\nconst\n { credits } = \nawait\n response.\njson\n()\n\nconsole\n.\nlog\n(credits, \n'credits'\n)\n\nBudget before a batch.\n Pictures and videos need their whole price in hand, and each is taken as it starts. Before you send many at once, check that your balance covers all of them: 16 credits a picture, about 256 a video. The same number, with your plan and your usage, is on \nSettings › Billing\n.\n\nLimits\n\nBoth reads are free. They count toward the key's 120 reads a minute, shared with checking on videos and downloading them. Past that the answer is \n429\n with a \nRetry-After\n header; see \nRate limits\n.",
  "api_videos": "API\n\nVideos\n\nFive second clips from a prompt, 1280 by 720. A video takes a minute or two, so it is made in the background: you start it, check on it, and download the MP4 once it is ready. The calls follow OpenAI's video API, so \nvideos.create\n, \nvideos.retrieve\n and the download in the OpenAI SDKs work with Creora.\n\n  \n\n5\n\nseconds a video\n\n  \n\n1280×720\n\nwidescreen MP4\n\n  \n\n~256\n\ncredits, given back if it cannot be made\n\n  \n\n3\n\nvideos being made at once for each account\n\nHow it works\n\n  \n\nPOST /videos\n\nqueued\n\n  \n\n  \n\nBeing made\n\nin_progress\n\n  \n\n  \n\nReady\n\ncompleted\n\n  \n\n  \n\nGET content\n\nMP4\n\n  \n\nStart it\n\nPOST /videos\n with a prompt. The answer comes at once: a video object with its \nid\n and the status \nqueued\n. Its price is taken now.\n\n  \n\nCheck on it\n\nGET /videos/{id}\n every five seconds or so until \nstatus\n is \ncompleted\n or \nfailed\n. \nprogress\n gives a rough percentage on the way.\n\n  \n\nDownload it\n\nGET /videos/{id}/content\n answers with the MP4. It stays on your account, so you can fetch it again later.\n\nStart a video\n\nPOST\n\nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1/videos\n\ncreora-video\n\nJSON with \nContent-Type: application/json\n, or the same fields as \nmultipart/form-data\n, which is how the OpenAI SDKs send a video. Up to 16 KB either way.\n\n  \n\nprompt\n\nRequired · string\n\nWhat happens in the clip, 1 to 1,500 characters once spaces at either end are trimmed. Describe the scene, what moves, and how the camera moves. It is used as you wrote it.\n\n  \n\nmodel\n\nOptional · string\n\ncreora-video\n, which is also the default. Any other name is refused with \nmodel_not_found\n. In TypeScript the SDK's types may only list OpenAI's own video models; since this field is optional, you can leave it out.\n\n  \n\nseconds\n\nOptional · string or number\n\nOnly \n\"5\"\n (or \n5\n), the default. Any other length is refused.\n\n  \n\nsize\n\nOptional · string\n\nOnly \n1280x720\n, the default. Portrait and other sizes are refused.\n\nA starting picture (\ninput_reference\n), or any file sent in the form, is refused with \n400\n and \nunsupported_parameter\n, naming the field. Other fields are ignored. With cURL, form fields look like this:\n\ncurl https://creora.ai/api/v1/videos \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n \\\n  -F model=creora-video \\\n  -F prompt=\n\"Slow waves rolling onto a black sand beach at sunset\"\n\nBefore the video is queued, Creora checks that your balance is above zero, reads your prompt for the two limits on \nImages\n (a refusal is \n400\n \ncontent_policy_violation\n and costs nothing), checks that fewer than three of your videos are being made, and takes the video's price. The answer is the new video:\n\n{\n\n  \n\"id\"\n\n:\n \n\"video_6ab9f2e2a4c1d07e5b3f9c12\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"object\"\n\n:\n \n\"video\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"model\"\n\n:\n \n\"creora-video\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"status\"\n\n:\n \n\"queued\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"progress\"\n\n:\n \n0\n\n,\n\n  \n\"created_at\"\n\n:\n \n1790571234\n\n,\n\n  \n\"completed_at\"\n\n:\n \n\nnull\n\n,\n\n  \n\"seconds\"\n\n:\n \n\"5\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"size\"\n\n:\n \n\"1280x720\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"error\"\n\n:\n \n\nnull\n\n}\n\nKeep the id.\n There is no way to list your videos, so the answer to this request is the only place a video's id appears. Store it before you do anything else.\n\nThe video object\n\nWhat \nPOST /videos\n and \nGET /videos/{id}\n answer.\n\n  \n\nid\n\nstring\n\nvideo_\n and 24 hexadecimal characters.\n\n  \n\nobject\n\nstring\n\nAlways \nvideo\n.\n\n  \n\nmodel\n\nstring\n\nAlways \ncreora-video\n.\n\n  \n\nstatus\n\nstring\n\nqueued\n, \nin_progress\n, \ncompleted\n or \nfailed\n. See \nStatuses\n.\n\n  \n\nprogress\n\ninteger\n\n0 to 100. While the video is being made it is an estimate from how long videos usually take, updated about every five seconds, and it stays at 95 or below until the video is done; when there is no estimate it reads 50. A completed video reads 100.\n\n  \n\ncreated_at\n\ninteger\n\nWhen it was started, in Unix seconds.\n\n  \n\ncompleted_at\n\ninteger or null\n\nWhen it was completed or failed, in Unix seconds; \nnull\n until then.\n\n  \n\nseconds\n\nstring\n\nAlways \n\"5\"\n.\n\n  \n\nsize\n\nstring\n\nAlways \n1280x720\n.\n\n  \n\nerror\n\nobject or null\n\nnull\n, unless the video failed: then \ncode\n is \nvideo_failed\n and \nmessage\n says why, and that its credits were returned.\n\nA video that failed looks like this:\n\n{\n\n  \n\"id\"\n\n:\n \n\"video_6ab9f2e2a4c1d07e5b3f9c12\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"object\"\n\n:\n \n\"video\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"model\"\n\n:\n \n\"creora-video\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"status\"\n\n:\n \n\"failed\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"progress\"\n\n:\n \n50\n\n,\n\n  \n\"created_at\"\n\n:\n \n1790571234\n\n,\n\n  \n\"completed_at\"\n\n:\n \n1790571290\n\n,\n\n  \n\"seconds\"\n\n:\n \n\"5\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"size\"\n\n:\n \n\"1280x720\"\n\n,\n\n  \n\"error\"\n\n:\n \n{\n\n    \n\"code\"\n\n:\n \n\"video_failed\"\n\n,\n\n    \n\"message\"\n\n:\n \n\"That video could not be made. Its credits were returned.\"\n\n  \n}\n\n}\n\nStatuses\n\nStatus\n\nWhat it means\n\nWhat to do\n\n  \n\nqueued\n\nAccepted and paid for, waiting to be picked up. \nprogress\n is 0.\n\nKeep checking.\n\n  \n\nin_progress\n\nBeing made. \nprogress\n climbs as an estimate.\n\nKeep checking, every five seconds or so.\n\n  \n\ncompleted\n\nMade and stored. \nprogress\n is 100 and \ncompleted_at\n is set.\n\nDownload it from \n/content\n.\n\n  \n\nfailed\n\nIt could not be made, and its credits are back on your account. \nerror.message\n says why.\n\nChange the prompt if it was refused, or start it again.\n\nThe messages a failed video can carry:\n\n  \n\nThat video could not be made. Try describing it differently. Its credits were returned.\n The clip was refused while it was being made.\n\n  \n\nThat video could not be made. Its credits were returned.\n It failed for good. Creora already retries passing trouble on its own, so start it again only if you still want it.\n\n  \n\nThe video took too long and was stopped. Its credits were returned.\n It was not done 15 minutes after it started.\n\nCheck on a video\n\nGET\n\nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1/videos/{id}\n\nAnswers the video object as it stands now. Any key of the account that started the video can read it; for any other account, and for an id that does not exist, the answer is \n404\n \nnot_found\n. Reading is free and counts toward the key's 120 reads a minute, so checking every five seconds leaves plenty of room.\n\nA video still marked \nqueued\n or \nin_progress\n 20 minutes after it started has lost its way somewhere on Creora's side. Reading it then marks it \nfailed\n and gives its credits back, and it no longer counts toward your three.\n\nDownload the video\n\nGET\n\nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1/videos/{id}/content\n\nAnswers the MP4 itself, with \nContent-Type: video/mp4\n and \nContent-Disposition: inline; filename=\"video_….mp4\"\n. Before the video is completed, the answer is \n404\n with the code \nvideo_not_ready\n and a message such as \nThat video is queued; its content is there once it is completed.\n\n  \nRanges work, so a player can seek: send \nRange: bytes=0-1048575\n and the answer is \n206\n with a \nContent-Range\n header. A range past the end of the file is \n416\n.\n\n  \nThere are no thumbnails or sprite sheets. The \nvariant\n option some SDKs offer is ignored, and the answer is always the MP4.\n\n  \nThe file stays on your account with no expiry date, so you can download it again whenever you like. It is deleted with your account.\n\n  \nA \n<video>\n tag in a browser cannot send your key. To show a video on your site, download it on your server and serve it from there.\n\nFull example\n\nStart a video, check on it every five seconds, and save it when it is ready.\n\n  \n\ncURL\n\nPython\n\nJavaScript\n\n  \n\n# 1. Start the video and keep its id (needs jq)\n\nID=$(curl -s https://creora.ai/api/v1/videos \\\n  -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n \\\n  -H \n\"Content-Type: application/json\"\n \\\n  -d \n'{\"model\": \"creora-video\", \"prompt\": \"Slow waves rolling onto a black sand beach at sunset, low camera, gentle drift to the left\"}'\n \\\n  | jq -r \n'.id'\n)\n\necho\n \n\"Started \n$ID\n\"\n\n# 2. Check every five seconds until it is completed or failed\n\nwhile\n \ntrue\n; \ndo\n\n  STATUS=$(curl -s https://creora.ai/api/v1/videos/\n$ID\n \\\n    -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n | jq -r \n'.status'\n)\n  \necho\n \n\"\n$STATUS\n\"\n\n  \ncase\n \n\"\n$STATUS\n\"\n \nin\n\n    queued|in_progress) \nsleep\n 5 ;;\n    *) \nbreak\n ;;\n  \nesac\n\ndone\n\n# 3. Save the MP4\n\nif\n [ \n\"\n$STATUS\n\"\n = \n\"completed\"\n ]; \nthen\n\n  curl https://creora.ai/api/v1/videos/\n$ID\n/content \\\n    -H \n\"Authorization: Bearer \n$CREORA_API_KEY\n\"\n \\\n    -o waves.mp4\n\nfi\n\n  \n\nimport\n os\n\nimport\n time\n\nfrom\n openai \nimport\n OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\n\"https://creora.ai/api/v1\"\n, api_key=os.environ[\n\"CREORA_API_KEY\"\n])\n\nvideo = client.videos.create(\n    model=\n\"creora-video\"\n,\n    prompt=\n\"Slow waves rolling onto a black sand beach at sunset, low camera, gentle drift to the left\"\n,\n)\n\nprint\n(\n\"Started\"\n, video.\nid\n)\n\nwhile\n video.status \nin\n (\n\"queued\"\n, \n\"in_progress\"\n):\n    time.sleep(\n5\n)\n    video = client.videos.retrieve(video.\nid\n)\n    \nprint\n(video.status, \nf\"\n{video.progress}\n%\"\n)\n\nif\n video.status == \n\"failed\"\n:\n    \nraise\n SystemExit(video.error.message)\n\ncontent = client.videos.download_content(video.\nid\n)\ncontent.write_to_file(\n\"waves.mp4\"\n)\n\n  \n\nimport\n { writeFile } \nfrom\n \n'node:fs/promises'\n\nimport\n \nOpenAI\n \nfrom\n \n'openai'\n\nconst\n client = \nnew\n \nOpenAI\n({ \nbaseURL\n: \n'https://creora.ai/api/v1'\n, \napiKey\n: process.\nenv\n.\nCREORA_API_KEY\n })\n\nconst\n \nsleep\n = (\nms\n) => \nnew\n \nPromise\n(\n(\nresolve\n) =>\n \nsetTimeout\n(resolve, ms))\n\nlet\n video = \nawait\n client.\nvideos\n.\ncreate\n({\n  \nmodel\n: \n'creora-video'\n,\n  \nprompt\n: \n'Slow waves rolling onto a black sand beach at sunset, low camera, gentle drift to the left'\n,\n})\n\nconsole\n.\nlog\n(\n'Started'\n, video.\nid\n)\n\nwhile\n (video.\nstatus\n === \n'queued'\n || video.\nstatus\n === \n'in_progress'\n) {\n  \nawait\n \nsleep\n(\n5000\n)\n  video = \nawait\n client.\nvideos\n.\nretrieve\n(video.\nid\n)\n  \nconsole\n.\nlog\n(video.\nstatus\n, \n`\n${video.progress}\n%`\n)\n}\n\nif\n (video.\nstatus\n === \n'failed'\n) \nthrow\n \nnew\n \nError\n(video.\nerror\n.\nmessage\n)\n\nconst\n content = \nawait\n client.\nvideos\n.\ndownloadContent\n(video.\nid\n)\n\nawait\n \nwriteFile\n(\n'waves.mp4'\n, \nBuffer\n.\nfrom\n(\nawait\n content.\narrayBuffer\n()))\n\nThe \nvideos\n methods need a recent version of the OpenAI SDK. If \nclient.videos\n is missing, update the package.\n\nPrice, refunds and timing\n\n  \nA video costs what it is quoted when you start it, about \n256 credits\n (about $0.26) for five seconds. The price is taken when the video is queued.\n\n  \nYou need a balance above zero to start, and then the whole price. With less, the answer is \n402\n with a message such as \nThat costs 256 credits and this account has 100.\n Nothing is queued.\n\n  \nIts credits come back, once, if it cannot be started (you get the error and no video), if it fails or is refused while it is being made, or if it takes longer than 15 minutes. The return is listed under \nCredits added\n on the billing page.\n\n  \nChecking on a video and downloading it are free.\n\n  \nMost videos are ready in a minute or two.\n\nLimits\n\n  \n\nVideos started, for each key\n\n30 an hour\n\n  \n\nBeing made at once, for each account\n\n3\n\n  \n\nReads, for each key\n\n120 a minute\n\n  \n\nLength and size\n\n5 seconds, 1280 by 720\n\n  \n\nPrompt\n\n1 to 1,500 characters\n\n  \n\nRequest body\n\n16 KB\n\n  \n\nLongest wait before it is stopped\n\n15 minutes\n\nA fourth video while three are being made is refused with \n429\n and \ntoo_many_videos\n, with \nRetry-After: 30\n. The 120 reads a minute are shared with the other reads on the key: models, balance and downloads.\n\nNot supported\n\nListing your videos, deleting one, remixing one, starting from a picture (\ninput_reference\n), other lengths and sizes, and thumbnails. A method a path does not take, such as \nDELETE /videos/{id}\n, is answered with \n405\n.",
  "building": "Create\n\nBuilding your app\n\nReact\n\nTailwind\n\nDatabase\n\nAccounts\n\nYou describe what you want and the agent writes the files while you watch. It checks its own work, runs the app beside the code and keeps a history of every change, so you can always go back.\n\nAsking for what you want\n\nThe box at the bottom left says \nDescribe what to build…\n. Type a request of up to 8,000 characters and press Enter, or press ⌘⏎ from anywhere in the box, and Shift and Enter makes a new line. On a new project the empty box offers three suggestions you can press instead of typing. The agent explains what it is doing in the transcript, every file it touches gets a row you can open, and the preview on the right reloads as the files land.\n\n  \n\ncreora.ai/editor/66f1c2a9b3d4e5f6a7b8c9d0\n\n  \n\nYou · just now\n\nA habit tracker with a 7 day grid that saves to a database\n\n  \n\n    \n\nList files\n\n2 files\n\n    \n\nDescribe schema\n\n0 tables · 0 rows\n\n    \n\nCreate table habits\n\n4 columns · change 1\n\n    \n\nCreate table habit_entries\n\n5 columns · change 2\n\n    \n\nWrite App.tsx\n\nOverwrote · 267 lines\n\n    \n\nCheck the project\n\nNo problems\n\n    \n\nLook at the app\n\nScreenshot\n\n  \n\n  \nThe habit tracker is built and saves to the database.\n\nHow the agent checks its work\n\n  \n\nRead\n\nList and read files\n\n  \n\n  \n\nWrite\n\nEdit with exact replacements\n\n  \n\n  \n\nCheck\n\nCompile the whole project\n\n  \n\n  \n\nLook\n\nScreenshot the running app\n\nAfter editing, the agent compiles the whole project and reports the result in a row called \nCheck the project\n. The check catches syntax errors, missing files or packages, undefined names, wrong component props and wrong tables or columns. If it finds problems when the agent believes it is finished, the agent is sent back to fix them, up to twice in one run. A \nLook at the app\n row shows a screenshot of the running project's first screen so you can see exactly what the agent saw.\n\nIf the preview shows an error after a run, press \nAsk the agent to fix it\n under the error and it is sent to the agent as a message of its own.\n\nThe editor\n\nThree parts, left to right: the transcript with the message box under it, the file tree, and the workspace. The buttons in the top bar swap the workspace between \nCode\n, \nPreview\n, \nAssets\n and \nHistory\n, and \nData\n. On a narrow screen the parts become a row of buttons along the bottom.\n\n  \n\nTranscript\n\nThe conversation with the agent, with the message box and the model menu underneath.\n\n  \n\nFile tree\n\nEvery file in the project. The plus button makes a new file, a path with slashes creates the folders, and the menu on a file offers New file here, Rename and Delete.\n\n  \n\nCode\n\nA real code editor that saves shortly after you stop typing, or immediately with ⌘S. Your hand edits and the agent's edits go to the same place, and the agent sees yours on its next run. It is read only while the agent is working.\n\n  \n\nPreview\n\nThe running app. If it does not compile, a panel along the bottom gives the reason.\n\n  \n\nAssets, History, Data\n\nUploaded images, restore points, and the database and its sign ups, covered in \nDatabases and accounts\n.\n\n  \n\nTop bar\n\nPush\n to GitHub, \nPublish\n, the coin button for a token, which shows \nSoon\n while launching is coming soon, and \nDownload\n. On a phone they sit in a menu.\n\nPhrasing a change well\n\nThe agent does well with a clear, bounded request and badly with a vague one.\n\n  \nAsk for one thing at a time, because three messages work better than one paragraph with three ideas in it.\n\n  \nName the thing you mean, such as \"the button in the header\", \"the second card\" or \nsrc/App.tsx\n.\n\n  \nPut the exact words you want on screen in quotes, and describe the result you want rather than the code.\n\n  \nSay what to leave alone, for example \"keep the layout, only change the colours\".\n\n  \nPaste, drop or attach up to four PNG, JPEG or WebP pictures per message when it is quicker to show than describe. A picture meant for the app, such as a logo, is given to it at a usable address straight away.\n\nChoosing the model\n\n  \n\nDefault\n\nClaude\n\nAnthropic's model and the default. It can run on your own Anthropic key, in which case it costs no credits.\n\n  \n\nComing soon\n\nMiniMax M3\n\nAn open model that will do the same work for about a twelfth of the credits. It will always cost credits, even with your own key.\n\nEvery run uses Claude today. Once a second model is offered, a model menu appears under the message box: it says which model your next request goes to, it is remembered for each project on each device, and it cannot be changed while a request is running.\n\nWhat a run can and cannot do\n\nA run reads, writes, renames and deletes files inside this one project and touches nothing else.\n\n  \nA project holds up to 200 files, each up to 200 KB, and 2 MB in total. The names \nindex.tsx\n, \ncreora.ts\n and \nsrc/creora.ts\n are reserved.\n\n  \nReact, an icon set and Tailwind classes are ready to use. Seven libraries install the moment a file imports them: \nrecharts\n, \ndate-fns\n, \nframer-motion\n, \nmarkdown-to-jsx\n, \nzustand\n, \nreact-hook-form\n and \nzod\n. Anything else the agent writes itself.\n\n  \nA run pauses after 24 steps or 60 tool calls and offers \nContinue\n, which picks the work up where it stopped.\n\n  \nWithout credits, a run uses one of your three free uses for the day and does as much as one free use covers. If it stops there, \nContinue\n starts another run and uses another. With credits, the run uses them instead and is not cut short.\n\n  \nOne run at a time per project, and 30 runs an hour.\n\nStopping and going back\n\nWhile a run is going, Send becomes \nStop\n and Esc does the same. Stopping is safe because everything written up to that moment is kept. \nHistory\n lists restore points labelled with what you asked for, each holding the files as they were before that change, and restoring is itself a point in the list so going back is never final. Creora keeps the last 20 points per project.\n\nIf the same project is open in another tab, Creora may say it changed somewhere else and ask whether to \nLoad that version\n or \nKeep mine\n.\n\nImages, downloads and deleting\n\nOpen \nAssets\n and press \nUpload\n, or drop files onto the pane: PNG, JPEG or WebP images of up to 4 MB each, with 40 per project. Each has \nAsk the agent to use this\n and a button that copies its address. The \nStore on Filecoin\n switch also keeps a copy with storage providers who prove on chain every day that they still hold it, with an IPFS link to open it and a Filecoin link to its proofs. The image is still served from Creora either way.\n\nDownload\n in the top bar gives you a zip of the project with the few extra files a computer needs to run it, and nothing in it ties the code to Creora. Deleting a project from its card in Projects removes the conversation, history, assets, published page and database with it, and cannot be undone.\n\nWhen a run is refused\n\nThe transcript says which it is, and links to the page that fixes it.\n\n  \n\nNo API key\n\nAdd yours under \nSettings › API key\n, or keep paying in credits.\n\n  \n\nOut of free messages\n\nWithout credits, each run uses one of your three free uses for the day, and they are used. More arrive at midnight UTC, and credits or your own Anthropic key keep you building now. As a guest, sign in and your account has its own three.\n\n  \n\nOut of credits\n\nA run on credits stopped when they ran out, and everything it finished is kept. Add credits on the billing page, or use your own Anthropic key for Claude. See \nCredits and API key\n.\n\n  \n\nFree build runs all used\n\nCreora gives a set number of free build runs each day, shared by everyone. Another is free tomorrow.\n\n  \n\nDaily budget used\n\nThe shared key has a daily ceiling that does not apply to your own key.\n\n  \n\nA run is already going\n\nWait for it to finish or press Stop.\n\n  \n\nRunning as many agents as it can\n\nCreora is at capacity for the moment. Try again shortly.\n\nWhen you are happy with it, \nPublishing\n puts it online.",
  "chat": "Create\n\nThe Chat\n\nWords\n\nPictures\n\nVideo\n\nBuild\n\nThe chat is where signing in lands, and you can use it before you sign in as well. It is a single conversation box that does four things: it talks with you, makes pictures, makes short videos, and opens the builder with your request already in it when you ask for an app.\n\nChoosing a mode\n\nType what you want and press Enter, or Shift and Enter for a new line. The row of choices under the box decides how your message is handled, and on an empty chat four example cards show what each one produces. A message typed in the box on Creora's front page is sent for you, in Auto, as soon as the chat opens.\n\n  \n\nDefault\n\nAuto\n\nCreora works out what you want, so a question gets an answer, \"make me a picture of\" gets a picture, \"a short video of\" gets a video and \"build me\" gets an app.\n\n  \n\nChat\n\nAlways answers in words, even when your message describes a picture.\n\n  \n\nImage\n\nAlways makes a picture from what you typed.\n\n  \n\nVideo\n\nAlways makes a video, and a separate Build option always opens the builder.\n\nPictures\n\nA picture takes a few seconds and appears in the conversation as a square 1024 by 1024 image, with a caption showing how Creora understood the request. Press it to open it full size in a new tab, or press the download button under it to save it. You can refer back to it in a later message, so \"the same fox, but at night\" makes a new version of that picture.\n\nVideos\n\nA video is five seconds long, widescreen and 720p, and takes a minute or two to make, while the conversation shows a card saying \nMaking your video\n. You do not have to wait on the page, because the finished video appears in the conversation whenever you come back. If a video cannot be made, the card says so and the credits it cost are returned, which you can confirm under \nCredits added\n on the billing page.\n\nBuilding from the chat\n\nAsking for an app, a website, a landing page, a game or a tool turns the chat into the builder. A new project is created with a name taken from your request, the editor opens, and your request becomes its first message so the agent starts straight away. The chat keeps a card for that project so you can reopen it later with \nOpen\n. Asking how something is built, or asking for code to read, is treated as a question and answered in words. The builder itself is covered in \nBuilding your app\n.\n\nYour conversations\n\nEvery conversation is saved as you go and gets its own address, so a reload or a bookmark returns you to it, and Creora gives it a short title after your first message. Your conversations live in the sidebar, the same sidebar every page of Creora has.\n\n  \n\nNew chat\n\nAt the top of the sidebar, and at the top right of the chat while the sidebar is closed. It starts an empty conversation.\n\n  \n\nYour chats\n\nGrouped by day: Today, Yesterday, Previous 7 days, Previous 30 days, then month by month. Open one and carry on where you left off, since Creora reads what came before. Each has a menu with \nRename\n and \nDelete\n.\n\n  \n\nYour account\n\nAt the foot of the sidebar, where you can sign out, or \nSign in\n for a guest.\n\n  \n\nLight and dark\n\nThe sun or moon at the top right of the chat switches between them.\n\nThe button at the top of the sidebar closes it for more room and the button at the top left of the chat opens it again, and on a phone that button opens the sidebar as a panel. Deleting a chat permanently deletes the pictures and videos made in it.\n\nPrivate chat\n\nPress the mask at the top of the chat, or \nPrivate chat\n under its greeting, for a chat that is saved nowhere: it never reaches the sidebar, its pictures go straight to your screen, and it is gone when you leave or start a new one. It talks and makes pictures; a video or an app has to be kept while it is made, so those wait for an ordinary chat. The details are in \nPrivacy and your wallet\n.\n\nWhat it costs\n\n  \n\n3\n\nfree uses a day, one of which can be a video\n\n  \n\nA few\n\ncredits for a message answered in words\n\n  \n\n16\n\ncredits for a picture\n\n  \n\n~256\n\ncredits for a five second video, quoted before it starts\n\nEvery day you get three free uses: each is a message answered in words, a picture or a video, and one of them a day can be a video. They start again at midnight UTC, and while you have no credits the line under the box shows how many are left. After those, the chat is paid with the same credits as the builder, which are explained on \nCredits and API key\n.\n\n  \nAsking for an app, a picture or video the chat will not make, and an answer that fails never use up a free message. Stopping an answer part way still counts it.\n\n  \nA free video that cannot be made gives its free use back, and a paid one gives its credits back.\n\n  \nIf you cannot afford a picture or a video, you are told the price and your balance and nothing is made.\n\n  \nWith no free uses left and no credits, a message is refused before it starts and your text stays in the box.\n\nWithout an account\n\nYou can use the chat before you sign in, as a guest: type in the box on Creora's front page, or open the \nchat\n. A guest has the same three free uses a day, and a \nSign in\n button where the account menu would be.\n\nAfter the third, a card asks you to sign in. Press \nSign in\n and whatever is in the box waits on your device, and once you are in it is sent in the same conversation. Everything you made as a guest, chats and apps alike, moves into your account, and a guest's chats are kept for a week if you never sign in. More on this in \nSign in and first project\n.\n\nWhat it will not make\n\nThe chat is open about the subjects it discusses and the media it makes, with two fixed exceptions. It refuses anything sexual involving someone under 18 in any form, and sexual pictures or videos of a real, identifiable person. It says so in a sentence, makes nothing and charges nothing.\n\nWho is answering.\n The assistant is Creora, built by the Creora team. Your messages are sent to the AI provider Creora uses to answer them, and to nobody else; see \nPrivacy and your wallet\n.\n\nWhen something goes wrong\n\n  \n\nCreora is busy\n\nToo many requests arrived at once. Wait a moment and press Try again.\n\n  \n\nThat could not be made\n\nThe request was declined. Change the wording and send it again.\n\n  \n\nToday's 3 free messages are used\n\nThey start again at midnight UTC. With credits, the chat carries on with them. As a guest, sign in and your account has its own three.\n\n  \n\nFree messages without an account are used up\n\nEveryone trying Creora without an account shares a daily amount, and so does everyone on the same connection. Sign in, free, and your account has its own three.\n\n  \n\nToday's free video is made\n\nOne video a day is free, and Creora also gives a set number of free videos a day shared by everyone. Another one is free tomorrow, or costs credits now.\n\n  \n\nThe chat is not set up here yet\n\nThis Creora has no chat. Everything else works, so start a project from \nProjects\n.",
  "community": "Resources\n\nCommunity\n\nCommunity in the sidebar is the forum where people building on Creora ask each other questions, show what they made and say what is missing. It also produces the ranks and badges on your public profile.\n\nReading and posting\n\nReading needs no account. Posting needs you to be signed in, and two short waits keep spam out: a reply needs an account at least fifteen minutes old, and starting a discussion needs one at least a day old unless you have already published an app. After that you can start five discussions a day and write thirty replies an hour. Discussions list twenty to a page with pinned ones first, sorted by Active, New or Unanswered, and every filter and page is an address you can send to somebody.\n\n  \n\nHelp\n\nSomething is not working, or the agent will not do what you want.\n\n  \n\nShow your work\n\nPost what you made, ideally with a link to the published app.\n\n  \n\nFeedback\n\nWhat is missing, what is awkward and what you would build instead.\n\n  \n\nAnnouncements\n\nChanges to Creora from the team. Only moderators start these, and anybody can reply.\n\nTitles are 8 to 140 characters, posts 20 to 20,000 and replies 2 to 10,000. Replies show oldest first, twenty five to a page. Posts support Markdown with highlighted code blocks, while raw HTML is shown as text, image addresses become links and links open in a new tab.\n\nIf you started a discussion you can mark one reply that is not your own as \nthe answer\n and any number as \nhelpful\n, and those marks are where standing comes from. You can edit your own posts afterwards, and they then show Edited. You can delete your own discussion only while nobody has replied; after that, ask a moderator.\n\nRanks\n\nThere are five ranks: Newcomer, Contributor, Regular, Mentor and Steward. Each requires a score, a number of separate days you posted, and a minimum account age, and all three must be met. The score comes from what other people did with your posts.\n\n  \n\n+12\n\nA reply of yours marked as the answer\n\n  \n\n+4\n\nA reply of yours marked helpful\n\n  \n\n+3\n\nA discussion of yours that somebody else answered, up to 20\n\n  \n\n+1\n\nJoining somebody else's discussion, up to 40\n\n  \n\n+1\n\nEach day you posted anything, up to 60 days\n\n  \n\n−15\n\nA post removed by a moderator\n\nThe score never goes below zero. The signals you can produce on your own are capped low enough that they cannot carry you past the third rank, so the top ranks are given to you by other people. How many discussions you started and replies you wrote are shown but score nothing. The forum's \nHow ranks work\n page shows your standing and the gap to the next rank.\n\nAchievements\n\nThere are twelve badges, earned from real work and never taken away. Examples include \nFirst project\n, \nFirst build\n, \nBuilder\n for twenty five finished agent runs, \nShipped\n for your first published app, \nThree live\n for keeping three apps published at once and \nSeven days\n for building seven days in a row. A badge is awarded the moment the thing happens. You see faded versions of the badges you have not earned yet, with what each requires; a stranger sees only the ones you earned.\n\nYour profile and Explore\n\nYour profile at \n/u/yourname\n shows your display name, picture, banner, bio, links, badges and every app you have published. Your wallet address is hidden unless you turn on \nShow my wallet address\n, and \nHide my profile\n makes the page read as not found to everybody but you. Both are in \nSettings › Profile\n; see \nYour account\n.\n\nExplore\n is the public feed of published apps, twenty four to a page, sorted by Newest, Most viewed or Most remixed. Every card has a \nRemix\n button that copies the app's code into a new project of your own, without the author's data, images or conversation, and you can remix up to thirty apps an hour. Publishing your own work is covered in \nPublishing\n.\n\nReporting and moderation\n\nAnybody signed in can report a discussion or a reply for spam, abuse, being off topic or another reason, with a note of up to 500 characters, once per post. Reports are not anonymous to moderators. Moderators can pin, lock, remove and restore posts, and a removed post keeps its place marked as removed so the action is visible.\n\nQuestions\n\nIf something is broken, or a moderator removed a post and you want to know why, start a discussion in Help on \nthe forum\n. The people who build Creora read it.",
  "credits": "Go further\n\nCredits and API key\n\nEvery account gets three free uses a day. Past those, the chat, videos, the builder, agents and the Creora API are paid in credits, unless the builder runs on an Anthropic key of your own. A credit is a thousandth of a dollar of AI work, so a thousand credits is roughly one dollar.\n\nWhat things cost\n\n  \n\n~770\n\ncredits for a typical build request on Claude\n\n  \n\n3\n\nfree uses every day before a credit is spent\n\n  \n\n16\n\ncredits for a picture in the chat\n\n  \n\n~256\n\ncredits for a five second video\n\nAn answer in words costs a few credits. A video is quoted before it is made, charged when it starts and returned if it cannot be made. You pay for what the agent actually used, including a request you stopped part way through. Long requests that read and rewrite many files cost more, and small corrections cost less.\n\nSee \nThe Chat\n, \nBuilding your app\n and \nAgents\n for what each one does.\n\nFree every day\n\nEvery account gets three free uses a day, and so does a visitor who has not signed in yet. Each use is one of these:\n\n  \n\nAn answer\n\nA chat message, answered in words.\n\n  \n\nA picture\n\nA picture made for you in the chat.\n\n  \n\nA build run\n\nA run of the builder when you have no credits, enough for an app's first version or a good change to one.\n\n  \n\nA video\n\nAt most one of the three each day.\n\n  \nThey start again at midnight UTC. Whenever you have no credits, the chat shows under its box how many are left.\n\n  \nA message counts once you send it, even if you stop its answer part way. Asking for an app, asking for a picture or video the chat will not make, and an answer that fails all give the use back, and so does a free video that cannot be made.\n\n  \nWhen a free build run has done as much as one free use covers, the transcript says so and offers \nContinue\n, which starts another run and uses another free use. With credits on your account the builder uses them instead and is not cut short.\n\n  \nAgents never use them: an agent's runs are paid in credits only.\n\n  \nCreora also gives a set number of free build runs each day, shared by everyone. When they are gone, another is free tomorrow, and credits or your own Anthropic key keep you building now.\n\nThe free plan has no monthly credits. Credits already on your account from before stay there until you use them.\n\nPlans and top ups\n\n  \n\nFree\n\nActive\n\n$0\n\n3 free uses a day, always there\n\n  \n\nStarter\n\nActive\n\n$12\n / 30 days\n\n12,000 credits, about fifteen builder runs\n\n  \n\nPro\n\nActive\n\n$40\n / 30 days\n\n40,000 credits, about fifty builder runs\n\nA plan is bought 30 days at a time and never renews by itself, because a wallet cannot be charged again on its own. Three days before the end the billing page says so, and \nAdd 30 days\n on your plan adds the next 30 to its end. After the end your account is on the free plan again, with every credit it still has.\n\nTop ups sell credits on their own, whenever you run low.\n\n  \n\n$5\n\n5,000 credits\n\n  \n\n$20\n\n20,000 credits\n\n  \n\n$50\n\n50,000 credits\n\nCredits never expire, whether they came with a plan or a top up.\n\nPaying\n\nYou pay in dollar stablecoins, or in Dash (see \nPaying with Dash\n). There is no card form and nothing to sign up for. Your account has a deposit address of its own, the same on every network below, and whatever arrives there becomes credits by itself, a thousand to the dollar: any amount, from any wallet or exchange, at any time, usually within a minute, or about seven minutes on Ethereum.\n\n  \n\nArbitrum\n\nUSDC\n\nUSDT\n\nUSDC.e\n\n  \n\nRobinhood Chain\n\nUSDG\n\nUSDC\n\n  \n\nEthereum\n\nUSDT\n\nUSDC\n\nTo buy a plan or a top up:\n\n  \n\nPress Buy\n\nOpen \nSettings › Billing\n and press \nBuy\n on a plan or a top up. The window that opens shows your deposit address and how much is still to arrive.\n\n  \n\nSend the amount\n\nSend that much or more to the address from anywhere. Or press \nPay $12.00 from your wallet\n, which names whatever is still to go: the wallet you sign in with, including the one made for you by Google or email, sends it for you, as long as it holds the stablecoin on the network you picked.\n\n  \n\nWatch it land\n\nThe window follows the money as it arrives and says \nPaid\n once the price is in, and your plan is on. You can close it at any time and pay later: what arrives still counts, and pressing Buy again picks up where it was.\n\nTo add credits without buying anything, press \nShow my deposit address\n under \nAdd credits\n the first time; after that it is always there. Copy it, send any amount, and the credits appear. The address is yours for good, so you can save it in your wallet or exchange and top up whenever you like.\n\nWhat you send is always yours as credits. Too little, and the window shows what is still to go. Too much, and the extra stays on your account: paying $45 for Pro gives you 45,000 credits and Pro for 30 days. \nPayments\n on the billing page lists every deposit, with a link to it on the network's explorer for Arbitrum and Robinhood Chain.\n\nPaying from your wallet also takes a little ETH on the network you pay on, for its fee: a few cents on Arbitrum, a fraction of a cent on Robinhood Chain, usually well under a dollar on Ethereum.\n\nOnly these networks and stablecoins count.\n Another token, ETH on Arbitrum, or anything sent on another network is not turned into credits, so check the network before you send.\n\nPaying with Dash\n\nYou can also pay for a plan or a top up in Dash. The first time you choose it, your account gets a Dash address of its own, which it keeps for good, and whatever arrives there becomes credits by itself, a thousand to the dollar like every payment.\n\n  \n\nPress Buy, then Pay with Dash\n\nIn the window that opens, press \nPay with Dash\n. It shows how much DASH to send for what is still to arrive, the rate it was worked out at, your Dash address and a QR code to scan with a Dash wallet.\n\n  \n\nSend it\n\nSend that amount to your Dash address from an exchange or any Dash wallet, or press \nOpen in your Dash wallet\n to have it filled in. If an exchange takes its fee out of what it sends, add the fee.\n\n  \n\nWatch it land\n\nThe window shows the payment as \nSeen, counting it\n, then \nReceived\n with the dollars and credits it became, and says \nPaid\n once the price is in.\n\nA payment counts as soon as Dash locks it with InstantSend, which usually takes a few seconds, or once it is in a block, and it is usually credited within half a minute of sending. Any amount from 0.0001 DASH counts. Too little, and the window shows a new amount for what is still to go; too much, and the extra stays on your account as credits.\n\nThe rate is the middle of the prices for DASH at that moment from Kraken, Coinbase, Binance and CoinGecko, of those that answer, taken only when at least two answer and agree within 2%. The window holds it for 30 minutes for the amount it shows: a payment sent in that time is valued at that rate, or at the rate when it arrives if that is higher. Anything you send above the amount shown, and anything sent after the 30 minutes, is counted at the rate when it arrives. When the time is up the window says so and offers \nGet a new rate\n. If you close the window and open \nPay with Dash\n again, it shows the same rate and amount while at least 5 minutes of the 30 are left and what is still to arrive is the same; otherwise it gives a new rate, for what you send from then on.\n\nYour Dash address takes DASH on Dash's own chain, and it is not your deposit address: stablecoins go to the one, DASH to the other. Since it is yours for good, anything you send to it later counts too, at the rate when it arrives: usually within half a minute if you opened \nPay with Dash\n in the last two hours, and within about five minutes if you did not. Each Dash payment is listed under \nPayments\n on the billing page.\n\nA Dash payment is public on the Dash chain, like any payment on a public chain: anyone can see it, with the address it came from and the address it went to. Your Dash address belongs to your Creora account, so Creora knows the payment is yours. Paying privately through NullMask is for stablecoins on Ethereum and does not cover Dash.\n\nPaying privately\n\nA payment to your deposit address is public on the network, like any transfer, and so is the wallet it came from. To keep that wallet out of it, pay through NullMask, a privacy pool on Ethereum: money that leaves NullMask cannot be traced on chain to the wallet that put it in.\n\n  \n\nHold USDT in NullMask\n\nOpen NullMask at \napp.nullmask.io\n, connect your wallet and shield USDT on Ethereum. USDT is the stablecoin its pool takes that Creora takes too.\n\n  \n\nUnshield to your deposit address\n\nIn NullMask, open \nUnshield\n, pick USDT, enter the amount and paste your Creora deposit address as the destination.\n\n  \n\nIt is credited as usual\n\nThe payment arrives like any other and becomes credits by itself, about seven minutes after it leaves NullMask, and a plan you are buying turns on once its price is in. Anything above the price stays on your account as credits.\n\nNullMask has costs of its own. It takes at least 20 USDT in and keeps half a percent of what goes in and of what comes out. The network fee for a withdrawal is paid from ETH you load into NullMask beforehand, about 0.012 ETH by NullMask's own estimate; on Ethereum that can be more than a plan costs, so check NullMask's figure before you start. New money waits about fifteen minutes in NullMask before it can leave, so a private payment takes around twenty minutes from start to credits. The \nBuy\n window has the same steps under \nPay privately with NullMask\n.\n\nNullMask is a separate service that Creora does not run. Your deposit address belongs to your Creora account, so Creora still knows the payment is yours; what NullMask hides is the wallet it came from. NullMask's own servers, which do the work behind its app, can see the transfer too.\n\nYour balance\n\nSettings › Billing\n shows where you stand.\n\n  \n\nFree messages and credits\n\nToday's free uses, the credits you have, and the plan you are on with the day it ends.\n\n  \n\nUsage\n\nOne row per day for the last 30 days: the credits it cost first, then the words in and out that made up the cost.\n\n  \n\nCredits added\n\nAnything that has been put on your account, and why.\n\n  \n\nPayments\n\nEvery payment to your deposit address or your Dash address, with a link to see it on the network's explorer for Arbitrum and Robinhood Chain.\n\nA request sent with no free uses left and no credits is refused before anything is spent, and your text is returned to the box. A request that runs out part way stops at its next step and keeps everything it finished, so your balance can end slightly below zero. The number is shown honestly, and the next credits added to your account settle it.\n\nUse your own Anthropic key\n\nThis avoids credits entirely for Claude requests in the builder, and it works today.\n\n  \n\nGet a key\n\nCreate one under API keys in the Anthropic Console at console.anthropic.com. It starts with \nsk-ant-\n.\n\n  \n\nSave it\n\nPaste it into \nSettings › API key\n and press Save. Creora checks it, stores it encrypted and never shows it again.\n\n  \n\nAdd a workspace id if asked\n\nSome keys need one, and a request tells you when yours does. Copy the identifier beginning \nwrkspc_\n from that workspace's address in the Anthropic Console and save it in \nWorkspace id\n. Leave the field empty otherwise.\n\nWith a key stored, every Claude request is billed to you by Anthropic and never costs credits. The Usage table still lists what those requests used, marked as paid on your key. The card shows which key is in use by its last four characters. \nRemove key\n switches you back to credits, and adding or removing a key signs your other devices out.\n\nThe Creora API\n\nYour own code can use Creora's chat, pictures and videos through the Creora API, which works with the OpenAI libraries. Make a key in \nSettings › Developers\n; it is shown once, when you make it. The API is paid in credits only, so the free uses and an Anthropic key of your own do not apply to it. See \nAPI overview\n.\n\nAgents\n\nAn \nagent's\n runs are paid in credits only, never with the free uses. Each run has a cap, \nMost credits a run can use\n, which is 200 unless you set another, from 20 to 5,000. When a run starts, its cap is held from your credits; when it ends, you are charged what it used and the rest is given back at once. A run never spends past its cap, and one you stop is charged only for what it did.\n\n  \n\nEach turn\n\n260 credits per million tokens in and 800 per million out, the chat's rate. Each turn reads the whole run so far again, so long runs cost more.\n\n  \n\nA web search\n\n16 credits, plus a little for reading the results.\n\n  \n\nPictures and videos\n\nThe chat's prices. A picture or video that is refused costs nothing.\n\n  \n\nPages, apps' data and notes\n\nNothing beyond the tokens of what they bring back.\n\nA run that cannot have its cap held does not start, and says so: \n\"This run needs up to 200 credits, and you have 120.\"\n A schedule's runs are held the same way, and a schedule pauses itself after 3 runs in a row could not start for lack of credits. What a run cost is shown on the run, step by step, and counted in \nUsage\n on the billing page.\n\nIf something goes wrong\n\n  \n\n\"You have used today's 3 free messages.\"\n More arrive at midnight UTC. With credits, the chat and the builder carry on with them.\n\n  \n\n\"This free run did as much as one free message covers.\"\n The app keeps what the run made. Send another message, or press Continue, to carry on.\n\n  \n\n\"Today's free build runs are all used.\"\n The shared free build runs for the day are gone. Another is free tomorrow; credits or your own Anthropic key keep you building now.\n\n  \n\n\"You are out of credits.\"\n Buy a plan or a top up, or use your own Anthropic key for Claude runs.\n\n  \n\n\"Paying with Dash is not available right now. Try again soon.\"\n Creora could not get an agreed price for DASH just then. Try again in a minute. A payment you already sent still counts, and is valued once there is a price.\n\n  \n\n\"The Anthropic API rejected that key.\"\n It was mistyped, or it has been revoked in the Anthropic Console.\n\n  \n\n\"That key needs a workspace id.\"\n Add the one for the workspace the key belongs to, as in step 3.\n\n  \n\n\"That workspace id was not accepted.\"\n Copy it again from the address bar of that workspace in the Anthropic Console.\n\nSee \nYour account\n for the rest of Settings.",
  "data": "Go further\n\nDatabases and accounts\n\nSome apps have to remember things, such as a list that is still there tomorrow, a booking, or a person who signs in. Creora gives a project its own database and user accounts without you ever writing a table definition.\n\nAsk for it in plain words\n\nSay what has to be remembered rather than how to store it, and include it in the same request as the screen that needs it. There is nothing to switch on first.\n\n  \n\"keep the items in a database so they are there when I come back\"\n\n  \n\"let people sign up with email and password\"\n\n  \n\"show each person only their own list\"\n\nThe agent creates a table for each kind of thing your app holds, and every row gets its own identifier plus the times it was created and last changed. If you asked for sign ups, it turns on account storage and writes the screens for them. A project can hold 25 tables, 50,000 rows and 100 MB, and the bars at the bottom of the Tables tab turn amber as you approach those limits.\n\nWhat your app can do with its data\n\n  \n\nRead and page\n\nFiltered and sorted reads a page at a time, with related rows from other tables brought along in the same request, so \"each comment with its author\" is one request.\n\n  \n\nCount and total\n\nCounts, sums, averages, minimums and maximums, in one row or grouped, so \"sales by month\" is one request.\n\n  \n\nWrite in bulk\n\nAdd, change or delete one row or every row a filter matches, and add or update on a unique column so a double submit does not create two rows.\n\n  \n\nWatch for changes\n\nKeep a chat, a board or a live count current as other people change it, without reloading.\n\nEvery one of these runs under the table's rules, and for a table that belongs to its users, only over the rows of the person signed in. Watching is no exception: a person watching such a table hears only about their own rows. A related table's own rules still apply when its rows are brought along.\n\nWho can read and write a table\n\nEach table has a four letter badge for reading, adding, changing and deleting rows, and hovering it explains who may do each. There are three possible answers.\n\n  \n\nOnly you\n\nEvery table starts this way, which makes it as private as your project.\n\n  \n\nSigned in users\n\nA table names the column that records whose row each one is, and each person then reads and writes only their own rows, invisible to everybody else using the app.\n\n  \n\nAnybody\n\nAnybody who opens the app. Normal for reading public content such as products or posts.\n\nBe careful with public writes.\n A table anybody may write is marked \nWorld-writable\n in a warning colour, because anybody who opens your app can add, change or delete its rows. Ask the agent to limit a table to the people signed in to your app and it will narrow the rule.\n\nFiles people upload\n\nAsk for it in plain words, such as \"let people add a photo to each item\", and the agent writes the upload and stores the picture's address in a column. Uploads are PNG, JPEG or WebP pictures of up to 8 MB each, with up to 2,000 files and 200 MB per project. Once the app has sign in, only people signed in to it can upload and each file remembers who sent it; before that, anybody who opens the app can, within those limits. A person can remove what they uploaded from inside the app, and you can remove anything from the Files tab.\n\nConnecting other services\n\nAn app can call an outside service, such as an AI model, a payment provider or a weather feed, without its key ever appearing in the app. Open \nData › Connections\n and add a short name, the service's base address, the key and who in your app may use it. The key is encrypted on save and never shown again; you can replace it or clear it, not read it. Calls pass through Creora's server, which adds the key, so the app only sees the response. 1inch and Blockscout are available as one press presets, covered in \n1inch and Blockscout\n.\n\nThen ask the agent in plain words, such as \"summarise each note with the openai connection\", and it writes the calls against that name. If it needs a service you have not connected, it says so and names the connection it wants.\n\nAnybody who can use your app can make calls through its connections on your account with that service, so keep connections limited to people signed in to your app unless the service is safe to share. A project holds up to ten connections, calls are limited to sixty a minute per app, and they can only reach public https addresses.\n\nThe Data pane\n\nOpen the project and choose Data in the editor. The database is made the first time you open it, so that first opening is the slow one.\n\n  \n\nTables\n\nEach table's columns, types and rules, with its rows below. Sort by a heading, load more, or delete a row.\n\n  \n\nChanges\n\nEvery change to the database's shape, numbered, newest first, each saying what it did and who did it.\n\n  \n\nAccounts\n\nThe people who signed up to your app, with Set password, Disable, Enable and delete.\n\n  \n\nFiles\n\nEverything the app's users uploaded, with who sent it and storage used.\n\n  \n\nConnections\n\nThe outside services the app may call, showing whether a key is set but never the key itself.\n\n  \n\nKey\n\nThe project key your app carries, its allowed origins and \nRotate key\n.\n\nUndoing a change\n\nEvery entry on the Changes tab has \nUndo\n. Nothing is rewritten: an undo is a new numbered entry saying which one it undoes, and you can undo that in turn. A row you delete from the grid lands here too, so one deleted by accident comes back.\n\nAn undo that could not bring the data back is never offered. A change that would destroy a table holding more than 10,000 rows is refused rather than made impossible to undo, and the message says to remove the rows first. Some changes cannot be undone, and the button says why rather than failing: turning on accounts stays on, because removing it would orphan every row that belongs to somebody.\n\nThe key your app carries\n\nThe project key is not a password. It ships inside your published app so anybody can read it, and all it does is identify the project, while what a visitor can actually do is decided by the table rules above. It is never in your files or your download.\n\nAllowed origins\n is a list of web addresses, separated by commas, that may use the key. Empty, the default, lets any page use it; filling it in stops a page somebody else built from using your key, and never blocks your own preview. \nRotate key\n issues a new key and stops the old one. Your preview and published pages pick it up within 30 seconds without republishing, while a copy you downloaded earlier stops reaching the database.\n\nThe people who sign up to your app\n\nYour app's users sign up inside your app with an email and password. They are not Creora users and have no access to your editor. Creora sends no email, so addresses are never verified and there is no reset by mail. If someone is locked out, use \nSet password\n to give them a new password of at least 8 characters and tell them yourself, which also signs out every session that account had. \nDisable\n switches an account off and stops its sessions, \nEnable\n puts it back, and deleting an account also deletes the rows that belonged to it.\n\nIf something goes wrong\n\n  \n\n\"This project has reached its database limit.\"\n You are at the table, row or size limit; the bars at the bottom of Tables say which one.\n\n  \n\nThe pane will not open.\n A Try again button appears with the reason.",
  "domains": "Create\n\nCustom domains\n\nA published app always keeps its Creora address, and it can answer on a domain of your own as well. You can connect a domain you already own, or take a free name under creora.ai that is live the moment you add it.\n\nConnect a domain you own\n\nThe project must have been published at least once, since \nDomains\n appears in the Publish dialog only once there is a site to attach a name to (see \nPublishing\n). You also need access to the DNS settings wherever your domain is managed, which is usually the company you bought it from.\n\n  \n\nAdd the domain\n\nOpen the project in the editor, press \nPublish\n, scroll to \nDomains\n at the bottom, type the full name you want (for example \nwww.pickle.cafe\n) and press \nAdd\n.\n\n  \n\nCreate one record\n\nAt your DNS host, add the single record Creora shows you and leave everything else alone.\n\n  \n\nCheck\n\nPress \nCheck\n. The domain reads \nWaiting for DNS\n with a note of what Creora actually found, and Creora keeps checking on its own for three days, so you can close the tab.\n\n  \n\nGo live\n\nOnce the record is found the status becomes \nCertificate pending\n for a few minutes while https is set up, then \nLive\n. Open your domain in a new tab and your app is there.\n\n  \n\nType\n\nCNAME\n\n  \n\nName\n\nwww\n\n  \n\nValue\n\nprojects.creora.ai\n\n  \n\nTTL\n\nAutomatic\n\nHosts call this page DNS, DNS records, DNS management, Advanced DNS or Manage zone. Choose \nCNAME\n as the type, not A, TXT or a URL redirect. Most hosts want only the part in front of your domain in the Name field, which some call Host, so for \nwww.pickle.cafe\n you type \nwww\n, and a few want the whole name. The Value may be called Target, Content or Points to, and the TTL can stay as it is. If a record with the same name already points somewhere else, remove it first, because two records for one name is the most common reason a domain never goes live.\n\nA name under Creora\n\nIf you do not own a domain, type a name such as \nmyapp.creora.ai\n in the same box and press Add. There is no DNS to set up and no certificate to wait for.\n\n  \nIt must be a single name with no dots in it, so \nmyapp.creora.ai\n works and \nmy.app.creora.ai\n does not.\n\n  \nIt must be at least three characters long.\n\n  \nReserved words such as www, mail and admin belong to Creora and are refused, and so is a name somebody else has already taken.\n\nA domain without www\n\nA bare domain such as \npickle.cafe\n usually cannot carry a CNAME record, which is a rule of DNS rather than of Creora, and the dialog says so when you add one. You can connect \nwww.pickle.cafe\n and ask your registrar to forward the bare name to it, or use a DNS host that offers an ALIAS, ANAME or flattened CNAME record pointing at \nprojects.creora.ai\n, which Creora will find.\n\nRemoving a domain\n\nPress the cross beside the domain in the list. It stops serving your app immediately while the site stays online at its Creora address, and Creora forgets the domain entirely, so you can add it again later or point it at another project. Remember to delete the record at your DNS host as well. Each site can have up to three domains, and each account up to ten.\n\nIf something goes wrong\n\n  \n\nStill waiting after an hour\n\nConfirm the record is a CNAME rather than an A record or redirect, and that its name matches. Some hosts take up to a day, and the note under the domain always shows what Creora found.\n\n  \n\nA different CNAME was found\n\nThis is usually a registrar parking page or an old host. Change its value to \nprojects.creora.ai\n and press Check.\n\n  \n\nAlready in use\n\nA domain can serve one Creora site at a time, so remove it in the other project's Publish dialog first.\n\n  \n\nLive but not found\n\nThe domain works but the site behind it is not published. Open Publish and check the badge says Live, or press Make live on the version you want.\n\n  \n\nThe name is refused\n\nIt has to be a hostname with no scheme, path or port, such as \nwww.pickle.cafe\n, not an address copied from the browser bar. Creora's own addresses are refused too.",
  "faq": "Resources\n\nFAQ\n\nShort answers to the questions people ask most, each linking to the page that covers it properly.\n\nSigning in and your account\n\nDo I need a crypto wallet to sign in?\n\nNo. You can sign in with Google, Apple, X, Discord, GitHub, Facebook or an email address, and Reown makes a wallet for you. You can also use a wallet of your own. Either way there is no password, and signing in costs nothing. See \nSign in and first project\n.\n\nCan I try Creora without an account?\n\nYes. Type in the box on the front page or open \nthe chat\n and you are a guest, with three free uses a day. After the third, the chat asks you to sign in, and everything you made as a guest moves into your account. A guest's chats and apps are kept for a week.\n\nMy wallet does not appear when I try to sign in\n\nThe sign in window lists MetaMask, Rainbow, Coinbase Wallet and anything that supports WalletConnect. If an extension is missing, unlock it and reload, because it only announces itself to a page that loads after it is awake. On a phone, open Creora in your wallet's own browser or scan the WalletConnect code. See \nSign in and first project\n.\n\nWhat am I signing when I sign in?\n\nA plain text message saying Sign in to Creora, with your address, the site's name and a single use code. It is not a transaction, so it moves no money, grants no permission and costs no gas. Cancel if it names a site other than the one you are on.\n\nI lost the wallet I signed in with\n\nThen the account cannot be recovered, because the wallet is the only key to it and Creora holds no password, recovery email or copy of your key. If you signed in with Google, another social account or email, the wallet comes back whenever you sign in with that account again, so losing that account or inbox loses the Creora account. See \nPrivacy and your wallet\n.\n\nHow do I change my username?\n\nIn Settings › Account. Your published app addresses change with it and the old links stop working, while the old name is held for you for 30 days. See \nYour account\n.\n\nHow do I delete my account?\n\nSettings › Danger zone › Delete account, then type your username to confirm. This removes your projects, data, published pages, images, profile and credits, and cannot be undone. What you wrote in the forum stays, with your name taken off it. See \nYour account\n.\n\nWhat does Creora store about me?\n\nYour wallet address, your username, whatever you put on your profile, your chats and projects, and what you have published. There is no password, and no email address unless you add one for notifications; an email you sign in with is known to Reown, which makes the wallet. Your wallet's key is never sent to Creora. See \nPrivacy and your wallet\n.\n\nBuilding\n\nThe preview is blank\n\nThe preview runs \nsrc/App.tsx\n and offers to create it if it is missing. A blank pane with no error usually means the component renders nothing. If there is an error, it appears in a panel at the bottom, and \nAsk the agent to fix it\n sends it to the agent. See \nBuilding your app\n.\n\nThe agent stopped in the middle\n\nA run ends when it finishes, when you press Stop, when it reaches the limit of one run, when a free run has done what one free use covers, or when the connection drops. Everything written so far is saved, so send another message describing what is missing and it carries on.\n\nMy change disappeared\n\nIf you were typing while the agent wrote, the editor asked which copy to keep. Otherwise open History and restore the entry above the one you lost, which is itself undoable.\n\nCan I edit the code myself?\n\nYes. The Code pane is a real editor that saves shortly after you stop typing, or with ⌘S. It is read only while the agent is writing.\n\nWhich model builds my app?\n\nClaude, made by Anthropic. A second, cheaper model is coming soon, and when it arrives a model menu under the message box lets you choose. See \nBuilding your app\n.\n\nCredits and paying\n\nWhat is free?\n\nThree uses a day for every account: a chat answer, a picture, a build run or a video, with at most one video a day. They start again at midnight UTC. There are no monthly free credits. See \nCredits and API key\n.\n\nThe agent says it needs credits or a key\n\nWith credits, runs use them. Without any, a run uses one of your three free uses for the day. With no free uses left and no credits, a run is refused before anything is spent and your message goes back in the box. Buy credits, wait for midnight UTC, or add your own Anthropic key in Settings › API key, which is never charged in credits for Claude runs. See \nCredits and API key\n.\n\nHow do I pay?\n\nIn dollar stablecoins: USDC, USDT or USDC.e on Arbitrum, USDG or USDC on Robinhood Chain, or USDT or USDC on Ethereum, or in Dash. Your account has its own deposit address in Settings › Billing, and whatever arrives there is credited by itself, a thousand credits to the dollar, usually within a minute. For Dash, press \nPay with Dash\n in the Buy window, which gives your account a Dash address of its own; a payment there is usually credited within half a minute, or within about five minutes if you send it without opening Pay with Dash first. Starter is $12 and Pro $40, each for 30 days, and top ups are $5, $20 or $50. See \nPaying\n and \nPaying with Dash\n.\n\nDoes my plan renew by itself?\n\nNo. A wallet cannot be charged again on its own, so a plan runs for 30 days and the billing page tells you three days before it ends. \nAdd 30 days\n adds the next 30 to its end. Credits you still have never expire.\n\nCan I use Creora from my own code?\n\nYes, through the Creora API, which works with the OpenAI libraries. Make a key in Settings › Developers. It is paid in credits only. See \nAPI overview\n.\n\nPublishing and domains\n\nHow do I get a link to share?\n\nPublish the project and the dialog shows the address it now lives at. That link works for anybody, signed in or not. For a name of your own, add a domain in the same dialog. See \nPublishing\n.\n\nMy published page says not found\n\nThat covers a wrong address, an unpublished project, or an old username. Check the address reads \n/u/yourname/yourslug\n with your current username, and open the publish dialog to see whether it is Live.\n\nCan people copy my app?\n\nYes, every published app can be remixed from Explore. Only the code is copied, never your database, uploaded images or agent conversation. If you would rather nobody copied it, do not publish it.\n\nMy domain still says waiting for DNS\n\nThe record has not been found yet. Changes usually spread within minutes but can take hours, and the dialog shows what it did find, which normally points to the problem. Press Check to look again; Creora keeps checking for three days. See \nCustom domains\n.\n\nWhat is a CNAME?\n\nOne line in your domain's settings, at the company you bought the domain from, saying this name is served by that other name. The publish dialog prints the exact record to add as its type, name and value. Copy those three into your registrar's DNS panel and change nothing else.\n\nMy domain has no www in front of it\n\nA bare domain such as \nexample.com\n usually cannot carry a CNAME at all; that is a rule of DNS, not of Creora. Use \nwww.example.com\n instead, or move the domain to a DNS host that offers ALIAS or flattened CNAME records. See \nCustom domains\n.\n\nData\n\nIs my users' data private?\n\nEvery table starts closed to everyone but you. When you open one you choose, per action, whether anybody or only people signed in to your app can use it, and a table can be limited so each person reaches only their own rows. Review these settings before publishing. See \nDatabases and accounts\n.\n\nWhat does the key in my app do?\n\nIt is a publishable key that tells Creora which project a request belongs to. It is not a secret, and what a holder can do is decided entirely by your table rules.\n\nTokens\n\nCan I launch a token today?\n\nNot yet on creora.ai: launching a token is shown as coming soon there. Tokens already launched can be viewed and traded on \nTokens\n. See \nToken Launchpad\n.\n\nDo I need money to launch a token?\n\nA little. It costs 0.0005 ETH plus gas on Robinhood Chain or Arbitrum, from your own wallet. Buying your own token is optional. See \nToken Launchpad\n.\n\nWhat is graduation?\n\nWhen the curve raises its target, about 3.96 ETH on Robinhood Chain or Arbitrum, it closes and everything it raised goes into a Uniswap pool at the same price, along with the unsold tokens.\n\nWhere is my creator reward?\n\nOn the token's page in a card called Your reward, when you are signed in with the launching wallet. It never expires and can only be paid to your address.\n\nIs there a presale or team allocation?\n\nNo. Everyone, including the creator, buys on the same curve at the same price, with no allocation, whitelist or presale.\n\nWhat is $CREORA?\n\nA token Creora plans to issue. There is no $CREORA token today, so anything offered under that name now is not Creora's. What it is planned to be used for is on \nToken Utility\n.\n\nGetting help\n\nWhere do I ask for help?\n\nPost in the Help category of \nCommunity\n with what you were building, what you tried and what happened, and paste any error inside a fenced code block. The people who build Creora read it. See \nCommunity\n.",
  "getting_started": "Getting started\n\nSign in and first project\n\nCreora turns what you describe into a working app in the browser with no setup. This page takes you from an email address, a social account or a wallet to your first project, and nothing else is needed: no money, no gas and no password.\n\nWhat you need\n\n  \n\nA social account\n\nGoogle, Apple, X, Discord, GitHub or Facebook. Reown, the company behind WalletConnect, makes a wallet for you and keeps its key in a page Creora cannot read. Your account is that wallet's address.\n\n  \n\nOr an email address\n\nType it, enter the six digit code sent to your inbox, and Reown makes a wallet for you in the same way.\n\n  \n\nOr a crypto wallet\n\nMetaMask, Rainbow, Coinbase Wallet and any wallet that supports WalletConnect all work, including wallets that live on a phone.\n\nSigning in is a signature, never a transaction.\n It costs no gas, moves no funds and approves no spending, and Creora never sees your private key or seed phrase.\n\nSigning in\n\n  \n\nOpen Creora\n\nPress \nSign in\n in the header of \ncreora.ai\n, or \nSign in\n in the chat. The card that opens says \nSign in to Creora\n. Press \nContinue with Google, email or wallet\n, which reads \nContinue\n on a phone.\n\n  \n\nChoose how to connect\n\nA window opens with Google and the other accounts you can use, an email field and a list of wallets. Pick an account, type your email and press Enter, pick the wallet installed on this device, or pick a mobile wallet and scan the code it shows with your phone.\n\n  \n\nConfirm\n\nWith Google or another account, sign in to it in the window that opens. With an email, enter the six digit code sent to your inbox. With a wallet, approve its request to connect to Creora.\n\n  \n\nSign the message\n\nThe message names Creora's address, the network, a single use code and the minute that code expires. Press \nSign\n. Nothing goes to a chain, so there is no fee and no wait, and the chat opens.\n\nTrying it without an account\n\nYou can use Creora before you sign in. Type what you want in the box on the front page and press Enter, or press \nStart creating\n: the \nchat\n opens and answers you straight away, as a guest.\n\n  \nA guest gets three free uses a day, the same as an account: an answer, a picture, a run of the builder or a video, with one video a day. They start again at midnight UTC.\n\n  \nAsk for an app and the builder opens on it, as it does for an account. It runs without a database, and publishing it, pushing it to GitHub and giving it a token wait until you sign in.\n\n  \nAfter your third use the chat asks you to sign in. Whatever you were typing waits on your device and is sent the moment you are in.\n\n  \nWhen you sign in, every chat and app you made as a guest moves into your account, and your account has its own three for the day.\n\n  \nA guest's chats and apps are kept for a week. Sign in to keep them for good.\n\nChoosing your username\n\nYour first signature creates the account with a generated name such as \nuser_3f9a1c2b\n. There is no form to fill in, and everything works under that name, although it is not one you would want on a page you share, so open \nSettings › Account\n, type a new \nUsername\n and press Save.\n\n  \nUsernames are 3 to 32 characters of lowercase letters, numbers, hyphens and underscores.\n\n  \nNames that read alike in an address count as the same name, so \nnick\n and \nn1ck\n cannot belong to two people.\n\n  \nYour username is your public address: your profile lives at \n/u/yourname\n and every published app at \n/u/yourname/project\n.\n\n  \nWhen you change it, the old name is held for 30 days so that only you can take it back, and links saved under the old name stop working immediately. That makes it worth choosing a name early.\n\nAround the sidebar\n\nThe sidebar on the left is the same on every page except the editor, the chat included. \nNew chat\n sits at the top, and under it you will find the rest of Creora, with the page you are on marked.\n\n  \n\nOverview\n\nYour dashboard. It shows today's free uses and your credits, offers four ways to start (Start with AI, Blank project, Templates and Import from GitHub) and lists your six most recent projects.\n\n  \n\nProjects\n\nThe full list, which you can search and sort by last change or by name, with Open, Rename and Delete on each card and \nNew project\n at the top.\n\n  \n\nExplore\n\nThe public feed of published apps, any of which you can open or remix into your own account.\n\n  \n\nCommunity\n\nThe forum for questions, finished work and announcements.\n\n  \n\nTokens\n\nThe launchpad, where a site can have a token of its own. Launching new tokens is coming soon on creora.ai, and tokens already launched can be viewed and traded.\n\n  \n\nDocs\n\nThis guide.\n\n  \n\nSettings\n\nUsername, profile, API key, developer keys, storage, appearance, billing, GitHub and the danger zone.\n\nBelow them are your chats, grouped by day, once you have some. At the very bottom is your account menu, with \nView profile\n, \nSettings\n and \nSign out\n, or \nSign in\n for a guest. The button at the top of the sidebar closes it for more room and the button at the top left of the page opens it again, and Creora remembers which you chose. On a phone the sidebar is a panel that button opens.\n\nYour first project\n\nThe quickest way is to ask for an app in the \nchat\n: it makes the project, names it after your request and opens the builder with the agent already at work. To start one yourself:\n\n  \n\nName it\n\nOpen \nProjects\n and press \nNew project\n, then give it a name of up to 80 characters. An empty name becomes \"Untitled project\", and the square button beside the field lets you pick an icon. Press \nNext\n.\n\n  \n\nPick a template\n\nChoose Blank (an App.tsx and a stylesheet), Todo app (a task list with filters), Landing page (hero, three feature cards, call to action and footer) or Dashboard (sidebar, four stat cards and a recent orders table).\n\n  \n\nStart building\n\nPress \nCreate\n. The editor opens with the chat on the left, the files beside it and a live preview on the right. From there, \nBuilding your app\n covers working with the agent and \nPublishing\n covers putting it online.\n\nIf something goes wrong\n\n  \n\nNothing happens\n\nYour browser may have blocked the window or your wallet may be locked. Unlock it and press the button again.\n\n  \n\nThe message expired\n\nThe code is only valid for a few minutes, so start again and sign the new message.\n\n  \n\nNo code arrives\n\nCheck the spam folder, then press Resend. An email code is valid for twenty minutes.\n\n  \n\nSignature not accepted\n\nCheck that the wallet signing is the one you meant to use. A smart contract wallet has to be on a network Creora can read, and the card says so when it cannot.\n\n  \n\nAsked to sign in again\n\nSessions last seven days, and adding or removing your own Anthropic key signs other sessions out. Sign in the same way and nothing is lost.\n\n  \n\nTemporarily unavailable\n\nThat is Creora's side, not yours. Wait a few minutes and try again.",
  "github": "Go further\n\nGitHub\n\nCreora can import a GitHub repository as a new project and push a project back as a single commit. You need a GitHub account and a personal access token that you create yourself.\n\nMake a token\n\nOn GitHub, go to \nSettings › Developer settings › Personal access tokens › Fine grained tokens\n and press \nGenerate new token\n. The GitHub card in Creora links straight there. Name the token, choose when it expires (Creora tells you when it is close) and which repositories it may reach, and give it the permissions you need.\n\n  \n\nContents: read\n\nEnough to import a repository into Creora.\n\n  \n\nContents: read and write\n\nNeeded to push a project to GitHub.\n\n  \n\nAdministration: read and write\n\nOnly needed if you want Creora to create new repositories for you.\n\nMetadata read comes with any of these on its own. Copy the token, since GitHub shows it only once, then paste it into \nSettings › GitHub\n and press \nConnect\n. Creora checks it with GitHub, stores it encrypted and never shows it again. The card then reads \nConnected as\n with your GitHub name, the last four characters of the token, the scopes GitHub reported and the expiry; \nReplace token\n swaps it for a new one. A classic token also works, although it reaches every private repository you can see and Creora cannot narrow it.\n\nImport a repository\n\n  \n\nChoose a repository\n\nPress \nImport\n on the Projects page or the \nImport from GitHub\n tile, then search your 300 most recently updated repositories or type \nowner/repo\n.\n\n  \n\nChoose a branch and check\n\nPress \nCheck what will import\n. Creora reads the repository without downloading it and shows how many files and bytes will come across, from which commit, and what is being left behind, grouped by reason.\n\n  \n\nImport\n\nName the project and press Import. An import always creates a new project and writes nothing back to GitHub.\n\nBecause a project holds at most 200 files of 200 KB each and 2 MB in total, and runs in the browser, a real repository rarely arrives whole. Build and dependency folders, lockfiles, images and other binary files, paths with spaces or brackets and oversized files are left behind, and application code is taken first. Nothing is rewritten to make it fit. If no main app file is coming across, the plan warns you, and the preview says so until you or the agent adds one.\n\nPush a project to GitHub\n\nPress \nPush\n in the project and choose an existing repository or create a new one, which is private by default. A repository the token cannot write to shows as Read only and cannot be chosen. Set a branch and a commit message and press Push. Creora sends one commit containing every file in the project and GitHub attributes it to you, so verified commits and organisation rules keep working. A push adds and overwrites files but never deletes anything in the repository, so workflows, licences and files you removed in Creora stay where they are.\n\nIf someone pushed to the branch since Creora last saw it, the push stops. You can choose \nPush on top of the latest\n, or \nPush to creora/<name>\n to put your commit on its own branch, and a link opens the branch on GitHub so you can compare first. A push is also refused while the agent is running, so wait for the run to finish and press Push again.\n\nRemove the token\n\nPress \nDisconnect\n in \nSettings › GitHub\n. Importing and pushing stop immediately and nothing already imported or pushed is affected. Delete the token on GitHub too if you want it gone for good.\n\nIf something goes wrong\n\n  \n\n\"GitHub rejected that token.\"\n It has expired or been revoked. Make a new one and connect it.\n\n  \n\n\"GitHub refused this.\"\n The token is missing a permission for that repository, or the repository belongs to an organisation that has to approve the token first.\n\n  \n\n\"GitHub is rate limiting this token.\"\n GitHub is counting requests; try again once its limit resets.\n\n  \n\n\"That branch has moved on GitHub since Creora last saw it.\"\n See the push section above.",
  "governance": "$CREORA\n\nStaking and Governance\n\nPlanned\n\nStaking $CREORA is planned to give holders a voice in how Creora develops. The people who build and earn on the platform would be the ones who hold the token, so governance would put decisions about the protocol in the hands of the people who use it.\n\nNot available yet.\n There is no $CREORA token today, so there is nothing to stake and no vote to take part in. The place to shape Creora now is the Feedback category of the \nforum\n.\n\nStaking\n\nHolders will stake $CREORA to take part in protocol governance. Staked tokens will represent a commitment to the ecosystem, and staking will be how a holder's position is counted when proposals are decided.\n\nHow a proposal will move\n\n  \n\nProposal\n\nA change to the protocol will be written up and published for discussion.\n\n  \n\nDiscussion\n\nThe community will review the proposal, for example in the Feedback category of the forum, and suggest changes before a vote.\n\n  \n\nVote\n\nStakers will vote on the final version of the proposal.\n\n  \n\nImplementation\n\nProposals that pass will be implemented and the outcome recorded publicly.\n\nWhat governance is meant to cover\n\n  \nPricing for AI usage, deployment and agent execution.\n\n  \nHow revenue settlement and ecosystem payments are handled.\n\n  \nSupport for new networks, models and integrations.\n\n  \nChanges to staking itself.\n\nStaking parameters and the governance portal will be announced on official Creora channels. Check \nOfficial Links\n for the current sources.",
  "integrations": "Own and earn\n\n1inch and Blockscout\n\nTwo services are built into Creora on both sides. The token launchpad uses them itself, and the apps you build can call them through a connection with your own key.\n\nBlockscout\n\nBlockscout runs the explorers for Robinhood Chain and Arbitrum, and Creora links to it wherever a chain is involved. On a token's page, \nContract\n opens the token on Blockscout, and every launch, buy, sell and claim confirmation carries a \nView on Blockscout\n link. Each token is a copy of the launchpad's own verified contract, so the \nVerified source\n badge opens code anybody can read.\n\nTo use it in your app, open \nData › Connections\n, press \nBlockscout\n and paste a key from the Blockscout developer portal, where the free plan is enough to start. One key covers every chain Blockscout serves, so an app can show balances, transactions, token transfers, contract source and token supply on Robinhood Chain, Arbitrum, Ethereum and more.\n\nExample prompt\n\nShow the last twenty transactions of the address the person types, with a link to each on Blockscout.\n\n1inch\n\n1inch finds the best price for a swap across every exchange on a chain, and it serves Robinhood Chain and Arbitrum as well as Ethereum, Base and other large chains. A graduated token's page trades in its Uniswap pool today. Quoting the best 1inch route beside the pool, and using the better of the two, is coming soon.\n\nTo use it in your app, press \n1inch\n under Connections and paste a key from the 1inch developer portal. Your app can then quote swaps, build swap transactions for a wallet to send, list a chain's tokens, read dollar prices and read a wallet's holdings.\n\nExample prompt\n\nAdd a swap box that quotes USDC to ETH on Base through the 1inch connection.\n\nWhat to know\n\n  \nThe keys come from each service's own portal and never leave Creora's server, which keeps them encrypted and adds them to each call, so they never appear in your app or its files.\n\n  \nBoth presets start limited to people signed in to your app. Open a connection to anybody only when the service is safe to share, since calls are made on your account.\n\n  \nCalls are limited to sixty a minute per app, and each service applies the limits of the plan you chose.\n\nConnections in general, for any service with a key, are covered in \nDatabases and accounts\n.",
  "limits": "Resources\n\nLimits reference\n\nEvery size, rate and count limit on Creora in one place, grouped by where you will meet it.\n\nProjects and the agent\n\n  \n\nProjects per account\n\n200\n\n  \n\nNew projects\n\n30 an hour\n\n  \n\nFiles per project\n\n200\n\n  \n\nFile size\n\n200 KB each, 2 MB total\n\n  \n\nMessage length\n\n8,000 characters\n\n  \n\nPictures per message\n\n4 (PNG, JPEG, WebP), 4 MB each\n\n  \n\nOne run\n\n24 steps or 60 tool calls\n\n  \n\nRuns\n\n1 at a time, 30 an hour\n\n  \n\nConversation kept\n\nNewest 2,000 messages per project\n\n  \n\nHistory\n\nLast 20 restore points\n\n  \n\nProject assets\n\n40 images, 4 MB each\n\n  \n\nProject name\n\n80 characters\n\nThe Chat\n\n  \n\nMessage length\n\n8,000 characters\n\n  \n\nMessages\n\n120 an hour\n\n  \n\nPictures\n\n60 an hour\n\n  \n\nVideos\n\n12 an hour\n\n  \n\nMaking a video\n\nUp to 15 minutes, then refunded\n\n  \n\nGuests\n\n3 free uses a day each\n\n  \n\nGuests on one internet address\n\n9 free uses a day together\n\n  \n\nGuest chats and apps kept\n\n7 days\n\nAgents\n\n  \n\nAgents per account\n\n10\n\n  \n\nRuns at once\n\n3 per account\n\n  \n\nName\n\n1 to 40 characters\n\n  \n\nDescription\n\n300 characters\n\n  \n\nInstructions\n\n8,000 characters\n\n  \n\nStanding task\n\n2,000 characters\n\n  \n\nTask for one run\n\n8,000 characters\n\n  \n\nApps per agent\n\n10\n\n  \n\nNotes\n\n50, of 500 characters each\n\n  \n\nOne run\n\n12 turns, 20 tool uses, 5 minutes\n\n  \n\nPictures and videos\n\n4 pictures and 1 video a run\n\n  \n\nMost credits a run can use\n\n200 by default, 20 to 5,000\n\n  \n\nLeast cap for paid tools\n\n40 for search or pictures, 400 for videos\n\n  \n\nWeb search\n\nUp to 10 sources a search\n\n  \n\nPages\n\nPublic https pages only, 2 MB read in 15 seconds, up to 3 redirects\n\n  \n\nWhat one tool brings back\n\n12,000 characters\n\n  \n\nApp rows\n\n100 read, 25 added or 1 changed at a time\n\n  \n\nAnswer kept\n\n32,000 characters\n\n  \n\nRuns shown\n\nNewest 50 per agent\n\n  \n\nRuns kept\n\n30 days\n\n  \n\nSchedule paused\n\nAfter 3 runs in a row could not start for lack of credits\n\nPublishing and domains\n\n  \n\nDescription\n\n10 to 200 characters\n\n  \n\nFiles to publish\n\nAt least 2\n\n  \n\nPublished versions kept\n\n3\n\n  \n\nPublishes\n\n20 an hour per account\n\n  \n\nCustom domains\n\n3 per site, 10 per account\n\n  \n\nDomains added\n\n10 an hour\n\n  \n\nDNS checking\n\nAutomatic for 3 days\n\nDatabases\n\n  \n\nTables\n\n25\n\n  \n\nColumns per table\n\n50\n\n  \n\nRows\n\n50,000\n\n  \n\nDatabase size\n\n100 MB\n\n  \n\nRow size\n\n64 KB\n\n  \n\nRows per read\n\n100\n\n  \n\nReads\n\n600 a minute per app\n\n  \n\nWrites\n\n60 a minute per app\n\n  \n\nApp sign ups\n\n20 an hour per app\n\n  \n\nApp sign in attempts\n\n5 a minute per email\n\n  \n\nApp user password\n\n8 to 200 characters\n\n  \n\nUser uploads\n\n2,000 files, 8 MB each, 200 MB total\n\n  \n\nUploads\n\n20 a minute per app\n\n  \n\nConnections\n\n10 per app\n\n  \n\nConnection calls\n\n60 a minute per app\n\n  \n\nUndoable change\n\nTables up to 10,000 rows\n\n  \n\nKey rotation\n\nPicked up within 30 seconds\n\nAccount\n\n  \n\nUsername\n\n3 to 32 characters\n\n  \n\nUsername hold\n\n30 days\n\n  \n\nSession\n\n7 days\n\n  \n\nDisplay name\n\n50 characters\n\n  \n\nBio\n\n300 characters\n\n  \n\nProfile links\n\n5\n\n  \n\nProfile picture\n\n1 MB\n\n  \n\nBanner\n\n4 MB\n\n  \n\nGitHub import list\n\n300 most recent repositories\n\n  \n\nGitHub imports\n\n10 an hour\n\n  \n\nGitHub pushes\n\n30 an hour\n\nCredits and the API\n\n  \n\nOne credit\n\n$0.001\n\n  \n\nFree uses\n\n3 a day, at most 1 a video\n\n  \n\nFree uses reset\n\nMidnight UTC\n\n  \n\nStarter\n\n$12, 12,000 credits, 30 days\n\n  \n\nPro\n\n$40, 40,000 credits, 30 days\n\n  \n\nTop ups\n\n$5, $20 or $50\n\n  \n\nCredits expire\n\nNever\n\n  \n\nUsage shown\n\nLast 30 days\n\n  \n\nAPI keys\n\n10 per account\n\n  \n\nAPI chat requests\n\n60 a minute per key\n\n  \n\nAPI pictures\n\n20 a minute per key\n\n  \n\nAPI videos\n\n30 started an hour per key, 3 in the making per account\n\n  \n\nOther API requests\n\n120 a minute per key\n\nThe API's limits and prices are covered in full in \nAPI overview\n.\n\nTokens\n\n  \n\nTokens per site\n\n1\n\n  \n\nName\n\n1 to 64 characters\n\n  \n\nTicker\n\n2 to 12 capitals and digits\n\n  \n\nDescription\n\n500 characters\n\n  \n\nToken picture\n\n1 MB\n\n  \n\nFirst buy\n\nUp to 20 ETH\n\n  \n\nPrice protection\n\n2% per trade\n\n  \n\nReferral code\n\n2 to 31 characters, 1 per wallet per chain\n\nCommunity\n\n  \n\nFirst reply\n\nAccount 15 minutes old\n\n  \n\nFirst discussion\n\nAccount 1 day old, or a published app\n\n  \n\nNew discussions\n\n5 a day\n\n  \n\nReplies\n\n30 an hour\n\n  \n\nTitle\n\n8 to 140 characters\n\n  \n\nPost\n\n20 to 20,000 characters\n\n  \n\nReply\n\n2 to 10,000 characters\n\n  \n\nReport note\n\n500 characters\n\n  \n\nReports\n\n20 a day\n\n  \n\nRemixes\n\n30 an hour",
  "links": "Resources\n\nOfficial Links\n\nThese are the official Creora channels. Announcements and product updates are published only through them.\n\nChannels\n\n  \n\ncreora.ai\n\nThe Creora app, where you sign in, chat, build and publish.\n\nOpen \n\n  \n\nThis guide\n\nThe step by step guide to Creora at creora.ai/docs, also under Docs in the sidebar.\n\nOpen \n\n  \n\n𝕏 @CreoraAI_RH\n\nAnnouncements, launches and product updates.\n\nFollow \n\nIn the app\n\n  \n\nChat\n\nTalk, make pictures and videos, and hand an app to the builder.\n\nOpen \n\n  \n\nExplore\n\nThe public feed of apps people have published.\n\nOpen \n\n  \n\nCommunity\n\nThe forum, for help, feedback and showing your work.\n\nOpen \n\n  \n\nTokens\n\nThe launchpad, where tokens already launched are listed and traded.\n\nOpen \n\n  \n\nBilling\n\nYour free uses, credits, plans and your own deposit address.\n\nOpen \n\n  \n\nDevelopers\n\nKeys for the Creora API, at \nhttps://creora.ai/api/v1\n.\n\nOpen \n\nStay safe.\n Creora will never ask for your seed phrase or private key, and signing in never requires a transaction. Treat any message that asks for either as a scam. Pay only to the deposit address shown in \nSettings › Billing\n on creora.ai. There is no $CREORA token today, so anything sold under that name is not Creora's. Confirm links against the channels on this page before connecting a wallet.",
  "network": "Own and earn\n\nGPU network\n\nRun a sealed GPU machine that answers requests for Creora's AI, and earn USDG on Robinhood Chain for every request it serves. Whoever runs the machine never sees what anybody asks: Creora checks the machine's proof itself, and sends nothing to a machine without one.\n\nOpening to providers.\n The node software and this guide are out now, so you can get a machine ready. Machines can be added in the \nconsole\n once the network opens on Creora's side; until then, the \nGPU network page\n and the console say it is not serving yet. Creora sends a machine work only once its proof checks out, so the network's model is served through the Creora API as machines join.\n\nWhat it is\n\nPeople with the right hardware run Creora's node software on their own machines. Each machine serves an open model, and requests to the \ncreora-network\n model of the \nCreora API\n are sent to one of them, sealed. The owner of the machine earns a share of what every request it serves costs. The chat on creora.ai, pictures and videos are not sent to the network.\n\n  \n\nSealed hardware\n\nIntel TDX and NVIDIA GPUs in confidential computing mode keep each request in memory that the person running the machine cannot read.\n\n  \n\nProof checked by Creora\n\nEvery 10 minutes Creora asks each machine for a fresh proof and checks it itself. No proof, no work.\n\n  \n\nPaid every day\n\nOwners earn 70% of what each request costs, paid once a day in USDG on Robinhood Chain once at least $5 is owed.\n\nWhy nobody running a machine sees what is asked\n\nA provider runs the hardware but cannot read what passes through it. Creora believes nothing a machine says about itself until Intel and NVIDIA have vouched for it, and checks everything on its own server.\n\n  \n\nSealed hardware\n\nThe machine is an Intel TDX host with NVIDIA H100, H200 or Blackwell GPUs in confidential computing mode, running Creora's node software in a confidential virtual machine. Its memory is sealed from the host and from whoever runs it.\n\n  \n\nProof Creora checks itself\n\nWhen you register a machine, and every 10 minutes after, Creora sends it a fresh random challenge and checks the proof that comes back: Intel's signature on the machine's quote, NVIDIA's attestation service on the GPUs' evidence, and that both answer this challenge and no other.\n\n  \n\nCreora's exact software\n\nThe proof carries the hash of the app compose file the machine was started from, and that file pins every image by digest and the model's weights by commit. Only hashes Creora lists are accepted, so a machine running anything else is refused.\n\n  \n\nSealed both ways\n\nEach request is sealed to a key born inside the machine, with a fresh key of Creora's own for every request (X25519, HKDF SHA256 and AES 256 GCM), and every answer is signed inside the machine with a key its proof also names. Creora checks the signature before it passes an answer on. Whoever runs the machine, its network or a proxy in front of it sees only sealed bytes, their size and their timing.\n\n  \n\nThe OS is checked too\n\nThe proof also carries the measurements of the operating system the machine booted, and Creora accepts only production dstack images it lists, measured for the machine's size. A development image, or an operating system of anybody's own, is refused, so the compose hash in the proof can be believed.\n\n  \n\nOnly Creora may ask\n\nCreora signs every request it makes to a node, for that node alone, and the node answers nothing else, so nobody else can use your GPUs or make your machine prove itself.\n\n  \n\nNo proof, no work\n\nA machine whose proof is missing, more than 15 minutes old or wrong in any way is sent nothing until it proves itself again. Nothing is taken on its word.\n\nWhat a machine needs\n\n  \nAn Intel TDX host: a 5th generation Xeon (Emerald Rapids) or a Xeon 6 (Granite Rapids), with TDX turned on in the firmware and a host kernel that supports it.\n\n  \nNVIDIA H100 or H200 (Hopper) or Blackwell GPUs, each with confidential computing mode on, passed through to the virtual machine, at most eight in one machine. Hopper systems that join their GPUs over NVLink in protected PCIe mode are not accepted yet.\n\n  \ndstack: its VMM on the host and a key management service, or a hosted dstack service that deploys Creora's app compose file exactly as it is.\n\n  \nA production dstack OS image Creora lists, never a development image, on a machine size (processors, memory and GPUs) Creora lists. Creora publishes the images and sizes it accepts beside its app compose file.\n\n  \nA public HTTPS address that reaches the node's port 8080, through dstack's gateway or your own TLS, passing paths through unchanged.\n\n  \nA clock kept right to within a minute.\n\n  \nEnough GPU memory for the model, and disk for its weights.\n\nTo see the GPUs' mode, \nnvidia-smi conf-compute -f\n must print \nCC status: ON\n, and \nnvidia-smi conf-compute -d\n must print \nDevTools Mode: OFF\n.\n\nSetting up a node\n\n  \n\nGet your owner id\n\nSign in and open the \nnetwork console\n. Under \nSetting up a node\n it shows your owner id, 32 hex characters. It names your account and grants nothing, so it is safe to give to whoever runs your machines.\n\n  \n\nGet Creora's app compose file\n\nThe node software runs from one app compose file, \napp-compose.json\n, which pins the node, vLLM and the model and carries Creora's server key as \nCREORA_SERVER_KEY\n. Ask for it on the \nCommunity forum\n. Check it before you deploy it: \nsha256sum app-compose.json\n must print one of the compose hashes the console lists, and the key inside must be the \nServer key\n the console shows.\n\n  \n\nSet your owner id\n\nGive the node your owner id as \nCREORA_OWNER\n, an encrypted environment variable only the virtual machine can read. It is the one setting you choose, so it is not part of the hash and every provider runs the same file. The node does not start without a valid one.\n\n  \n\nStart it under dstack\n\nDeploy the file exactly as it is, on a production dstack OS image and a machine size Creora lists, with the GPUs passed through, and allow its compose hash in your key management service. Once it is up, \n/health\n on its address answers \n{\"ready\":true,\"gpu\":\"ready\",\"vllm\":true}\n. Loading the model can take many minutes the first time.\n\n  \n\nRegister it\n\nIn the console, under \nNodes\n, give the node a name and its HTTPS address, such as \nhttps://node.example.com\n, and press \nAdd node\n. Creora checks its proof at once, which can take up to a minute, and shows the result. An account can register up to 10 nodes.\n\n  \n\nSet your payout address\n\nUnder \nPayout address\n, give a wallet address you control on Robinhood Chain. Earnings are kept until you do.\n\nChange nothing in the file.\n A compose file changed by a single byte, rebuilt by a hosted service with settings of its own, or started with another key, has another hash, and Creora refuses the machine.\n\nChecks and statuses\n\nEach check sends the node a fresh challenge and passes only when all of these hold:\n\n  \nThe owner id in the proof is the one in your console, and the quote binds the node's two public keys, its GPU evidence, that owner id and this challenge, so the keys were made in this machine and it works for one account only.\n\n  \nThe machine's TDX quote verifies against Intel's collateral, and its platform is up to date.\n\n  \ndstack's event log replays to the quote and names a compose hash Creora lists for the model the node serves.\n\n  \nThe operating system the machine booted is a production dstack image Creora lists, for the machine's size.\n\n  \nWhen Creora lists the key management services it trusts, the machine's disk keys come from one of them.\n\n  \nNVIDIA's attestation service accepts the GPUs' evidence for the same challenge, and finds every GPU measured as NVIDIA expects, booted securely and with its debug facilities off.\n\nCreora holds every node's last proof to what it accepts each time it sends work, so a node whose operating system or platform it stops accepting is sent nothing from that moment.\n\nThe console shows each node's status, the model and GPU its last proof named, when it was last checked, and, when it is not being sent work, why.\n\nStatus\n\nWhat it means\n\nChecked again\n\n  \n\nPending\n\nNot checked yet, or its last proof is too old to trust. It is sent no work.\n\nWithin minutes\n\n  \n\nAttested\n\nIts proof holds, and it is sent work. If a check could not finish because the node was busy or slow to answer, it keeps its work while its last proof holds, and the console says it is being checked again.\n\nEvery 10 minutes, or within 2 minutes when a check could not finish\n\n  \n\nOffline\n\nIt could not be reached, answered with an error other than busy, said its GPUs or the machine were not ready, broke off a request, or was too busy to take three requests in a row. Most often it restarted.\n\nAfter 2 minutes\n\n  \n\nFailed\n\nA check refused it. The console says which, in a sentence.\n\nAfter 30 minutes\n\nCheck now\n beside a node checks it again at once, at most once a minute for each node and ten checks a minute for your account, registrations included. A node that has not passed a check in the week since it was added is no longer checked on its own; press \nCheck now\n once it is ready, which gives it another week. A node that cannot be reached when a request arrives, or stops answering in the middle of one, is marked offline, and the next check decides when it is sent work again. A node that answers it is busy (503, 504 or 429) is not marked anything: it is sent no new requests for a minute and stays attested, unless it answers three requests in a row that way, when it is marked offline. Creora sends one node at most eight requests at once. A request a node could not take is tried on another node.\n\nEarnings and payouts\n\n  \n\n70%\n\nof what each request your nodes serve costs\n\n  \n\n$5\n\nowed is the least paid out, once a day\n\nEach request to \ncreora-network\n is charged to whoever made it, in credits at the model's price. The owner of the node that served it earns 70% of that, counted in dollars at a thousand credits to the dollar. The console shows what your nodes have earned, what has been paid and what is still owed.\n\nOnce a day Creora pays every owner who is owed at least $5, in USDG, Paxos's Global Dollar, on Robinhood Chain, to the payout address set in the console. Each payout is listed in the console with its amount, its status and a link to its transaction on Robinhood Chain's explorer. A payout that fails is listed with the reason, and its earnings are owed again and paid with the next one.\n\nWhen payouts wait, the console says why: Creora has not opened payouts yet, you have not set a payout address, or Creora's payouts wallet needs topping up. What is owed is kept meanwhile.\n\nEarnings belong to your account, whatever becomes of the node that earned them: removing a node does not lose them. If you delete your account, earnings not yet paid are forfeited.\n\nIf a node is refused\n\n  \n\nCreora's signed requests were refused\n\nThe node answered Creora's signed request with 401. Creora signs each request for the account it asks for, so most often \nCREORA_OWNER\n on the node is another account's owner id: set it to the one in your console and restart the node. Otherwise its clock is more than a minute off, or something in front of it, a gateway or a proxy, changes the path or the query. Keep the host's clock synchronized, pass paths through unchanged, then press \nCheck now\n.\n\n  \n\nBeing checked again\n\nThe node was busy, or slow to answer, when Creora checked it. It keeps its work while its last proof holds, and Creora asks again within a couple of minutes. If it keeps happening, check that the machine is not short of processors or memory.\n\n  \n\nOffline after a restart\n\nThe node makes new keys every time it starts, so requests sealed to the old ones fail and it is marked offline. Creora checks it again within a couple of minutes, or at once when you press \nCheck now\n.\n\n  \n\nSet up for another account\n\nThe owner id on the node is not yours. Set \nCREORA_OWNER\n to the owner id in your console, restart the node, then press \nCheck now\n.\n\n  \n\nThe compose hash is not one Creora allows\n\nThe machine was started from a file other than Creora's app compose file, a changed copy of it, or one Creora no longer lists. Deploy the file whose hash the console lists, exactly as it is.\n\n  \n\nThe OS measurements are not accepted\n\nThe machine booted an operating system Creora does not list, a development image, or a listed image on a machine size Creora has not measured. Run a production dstack image Creora lists, on a listed size, then press \nCheck now\n.\n\n  \n\nThe key provider is not trusted\n\nThe machine's disk keys come from a key management service Creora does not list. Point the machine at one Creora lists.\n\n  \n\nCreora stopped checking it on its own\n\nThe node has not passed a check in the week since it was added. Fix what its last reason said, then press \nCheck now\n.\n\n  \n\nThe TDX platform is not accepted\n\nIntel's collateral says the machine's platform needs an update. Update the host's firmware and microcode, then check again.\n\n  \n\nNVIDIA did not trust the GPUs\n\nA GPU is not in confidential computing mode, is in devtools mode, or its evidence was refused. Check the GPUs' mode as shown under \nWhat a machine needs\n.\n\n  \n\nCould not be reached\n\nCheck that \n/health\n on the node's HTTPS address answers from outside your network. Creora does not follow redirects, so register the address the node serves at.\n\n  \n\nAlready registered as another node\n\nOne machine is one node, whoever registered it. Remove the other entry to register it here. An address can be registered once by each account: someone else registering your node's address gets nothing from it, and does not stop you registering it.\n\n  \n\nThe network is not open yet\n\nCreora has not opened the network on its side, so nodes cannot be added or checked. The console says so until it opens.",
  "privacy": "Getting started\n\nPrivacy and your wallet\n\nWhat you make on Creora stays yours. The platform runs no analytics and keeps only the information it needs in order to work, and your account is tied to a wallet you control rather than to a password Creora holds.\n\nPrivacy principles\n\n  \n\nNo tracking\n\nThere are no analytics, no ad pixels and no third party measurement, and the wallet connection library runs with its analytics off. The server keeps request ids and errors, and it does not keep watch on what you make.\n\n  \n\nNot trained on you\n\nCreora trains no model on what you say or make. Your chats are kept so you can come back to them, and for nothing else, and a private chat is not kept at all.\n\n  \n\nThe AI never learns who asks\n\nYour requests reach the AI from Creora's server, without your account, your address, your IP address or your browser.\n\n  \n\nNo password\n\nYou sign in with a wallet, or with an email or social account that Reown turns into a wallet, and Creora never receives or stores a key.\n\n  \n\nPrivate until you publish\n\nA project and your chats are visible only to you. A project becomes public when you publish it, and you can take it down again whenever you like.\n\n  \n\nDelete means delete\n\nDeleting your account removes your projects, their files and conversations, your chats, your pictures, your agents with their notes and runs, your published pages and any key you stored. Copies you chose to keep on Filecoin are the one exception, as explained below.\n\nWhat Creora stores about you\n\n  \n\nStored\n\nYour wallet address and the network you signed in on, your username, whatever you put on your profile, your projects and their files, your conversations with the builder, your chats with the pictures and videos made in them, the images you upload, what you have published, and your credits and payments. An Anthropic key you add is stored encrypted and never shown again.\n\n  \n\nNot stored\n\nA password or your wallet's key. An email or social account you sign in with goes to Reown, which makes the wallet, and Creora only ever sees that wallet's address. A notification email is kept only if you add one in Settings, and it can never sign you in.\n\n  \n\nLogged by the server\n\nRequest ids and errors, which are used to keep the service running, and the name of a browser that reports a blocked script. Never what you write, ask or make, and no log of the pages you visit.\n\n  \n\nBefore you sign in\n\nA guest's chats, kept the same way as an account's, and a keyed one way hash of your network address, so that the free uses guests share stay fair. The address itself is not stored.\n\n  \n\nThe Creora API\n\nFor each call, the tokens or the price and which of your keys made it. Not your messages, and not the pictures it sends back. A video's prompt and file are kept so you can fetch it, until you delete your account.\n\n  \n\nAgents\n\nEach agent you make, with its instructions, standing task, tools, apps, schedule and notes, until you delete it. Its runs are kept for 30 days, each with its task, its steps and its answer. The pictures and videos its runs make stay with your account until you delete your account. The server's logs keep ids and numbers about a run, never what an agent was asked or what it found.\n\n  \n\nPayments\n\nEach deposit's network, transaction and sending address, which are public on the network anyway. To keep your own wallet out of it, see \nPaying privately\n. A Dash payment is public on the Dash chain, like any payment on a public chain: Creora keeps its transaction, its amount and the Dash address it gave you, and paying privately does not cover Dash. See \nPaying with Dash\n.\n\n  \n\nSent to answer the chat\n\nYour message and the recent part of that conversation go to the AI provider Creora uses to answer it and to make the pictures and videos you ask for.\n\n  \n\nSent to run the builder\n\nThe conversation and the files of that project go to Anthropic when the builder runs, on your own key when you have stored one.\n\n  \n\nSent to run an agent\n\nIts instructions, its notes, the task and what its tools found go to the AI provider Creora uses, from Creora's server, without your account or your address. Its web searches, pictures and videos go through the same AI provider. Pages an agent reads are fetched by Creora's server, so the sites see Creora, never you.\n\n  \n\nCopies on Filecoin\n\nOff unless you turn it on in \nSettings › Storage\n. A copy made there is public to anyone who has its address, and it stays on Filecoin after you delete the upload or your account, so keep it for pictures you mean to share.\n\nPrivate chat\n\nA private chat is kept nowhere. Press the mask at the top of the chat, or \nPrivate chat\n under its greeting, and the chat that opens is saved by nobody: no conversation, no messages, no title, and pictures that are sent straight to your screen instead of being stored. The page is the only place it exists, so it is gone when you leave, reload or start a new chat, and it never appears in the sidebar.\n\n  \n\nWhat it can do.\n Talk and make pictures, in Auto, Chat or Image. A video and an app both have to be kept while they are made, so a private chat says so instead, and the free use goes back.\n\n  \n\nWhat is still counted.\n Your free uses and credits, as numbers: how many tokens or which picture price, never the words.\n\n  \n\nWhat it sends.\n Each message carries the recent part of the chat from your page, because the server has none of it to read. It goes to the AI the same way any chat does, without your account or your address.\n\nTo leave, press the mask again or \nPrivate chat is on\n.\n\nYour wallet is your only way in\n\nYour account is your wallet address, so there is no password to reset and no recovery process. Nobody at Creora can let you back in without the wallet, which means you should keep your recovery phrase somewhere safe. If you signed in with email or a social account, the wallet Reown made for you comes back whenever you sign in with that address or account, so whoever can get into it can sign in as you, and it deserves the same care as a wallet.\n\nCreora asks your wallet to sign a transaction in only two places, and each of those transactions is one you start yourself: the \nToken Launchpad\n, and the button on the billing page that pays for a plan or credits from your wallet, such as \nPay $12.00 from your wallet\n (see \nCredits and API key\n).\n\nWhat the chat will not make\n\nThe chat is open about the subjects it discusses and the media it makes, with two fixed exceptions. It refuses anything sexual involving someone under 18 in any form, and it refuses sexual pictures or videos of a real, identifiable person. A refusal is explained in a sentence and costs nothing.\n\nFor the full legal terms, read the \nprivacy policy\n and the \nterms of service\n on creora.ai.",
  "publishing": "Create\n\nPublishing\n\nPublishing takes a copy of your project as it stands and puts it at an address anybody can open. You keep editing afterwards, and visitors see exactly what you published until you publish again.\n\nPublishing for the first time\n\nPress \nPublish\n in the editor's top bar and write a description of 10 to 200 characters, which visitors read under your app's name. The \nBefore publishing\n list then shows five requirements and ticks the ones you have met.\n\n  \nA name of its own, since \"Untitled project\" does not count. Click the name in the top bar to rename it.\n\n  \nAt least 2 files\n\n  \nAn entry file at \nsrc/App.tsx\n, the file your app starts from\n\n  \nA description of 10 to 200 characters\n\n  \nAt least one agent run\n\nThe app also has to build. Creora publishes what the preview in your browser has confirmed works, so it may ask you to \nRun the preview\n first, and it will show you any error instead of publishing. Once everything is met the list disappears and the Publish button lights up.\n\nPublishing needs an account.\n An app made as a guest can be built and previewed, and publishing it waits until you sign in. Everything you made as a guest moves into your account when you do.\n\nYour public address\n\nA published app lives at \n/u/yourname/project\n. The short project name is fixed when the project is created, so renaming the project changes the title people read without moving the address, while changing your username does move it. The dialog shows the full link with \nCopy link\n, \nVisit\n and a \nLive\n badge, along with a rough count of views that counts a visitor once per visit rather than once per reload.\n\nThe cover picture\n\nAfter every publish Creora opens your page and photographs the first screen, usually within a few seconds, to use as the cover on Explore and your profile, and the dialog says \ntaking a picture…\n until it lands. \nRetake\n asks for a fresh photograph, \nUpload\n uses a picture of your own that is never replaced automatically, and \nUse the generated one\n draws a cover from your project icon. None of these require publishing again. If your Creora has no browser to photograph with, the dialog says so and offers Upload only.\n\nUpdating and versions\n\n  \n\nKeep building\n\nYour public page does not change while you work.\n\n  \n\nUpdate to this version\n\nOpen Publish again and press \nUpdate to this version\n. Creora freezes the files, numbers the new version and points your address at it, and visitors see it on their next load.\n\n  \n\nSwitch back if needed\n\nUnder \nVersions\n, newest first, press \nMake live\n on any earlier version to switch the public page to it instantly. Nothing is rebuilt, and Creora keeps the last three versions.\n\nTaking it down\n\nPress \nUnpublish\n and confirm. The page stops working and leaves Explore and search, but nothing is deleted, so your project, files and versions are all still there and publishing again takes one press.\n\nWhat visitors see\n\nVisitors see your app and nothing else, because Creora adds no bar, buttons or branding around it. The tab carries your app's name and the page carries your name and description for link previews, so a shared link shows a proper card in chat apps and on social sites.\n\nEvery live site is also listed on \nExplore\n, where people sort by newest, most viewed or most remixed, and it turns up in search. \nRemix\n on its card there copies your published files into a project of the visitor's own. A remix copies only the files of the live version, and never your database, uploaded images or conversation with the agent. For an address of your own, see \nCustom domains\n.\n\nIf something goes wrong\n\n  \n\nPublish will not press\n\nSomething on the Before publishing list is unmet, or the preview has not built these files yet. Both say which.\n\n  \n\nChanged somewhere else\n\nThe project is open in another tab. Choose which copy to keep, then publish again.\n\n  \n\nRefused while the agent works\n\nWait for the run to finish, since a half written change is not worth freezing.\n\n  \n\nToo many publishes\n\nThere is a ceiling of 20 publishes an hour per account, shared with making old versions live again.\n\n  \n\nA visitor sees nothing\n\nCheck the dialog still says \nLive\n, and that they have the whole address, including your username.",
  "referrals": "Own and earn\n\nReferrals\n\nA referral code earns you a share of the launchpad fee on every curve trade made by the people you bring in. Codes are registered separately on each chain, and the Referrals page has a tab for each chain.\n\nSetting up a code\n\n  \n\nRegister\n\nGo to \nTokens › Referrals\n, sign in with your wallet, pick a chain and register a code of 2 to 31 letters, digits or underscores. Each wallet gets one permanent code per chain.\n\n  \n\nShare your link\n\nThe page gives you a link of the form \n/tokens?ref=yourword&chain=<id>\n. The chain is part of the link because a code on one chain means nothing on another.\n\n  \n\nEarn\n\nAnyone who arrives through your link and then trades on that chain is recorded as yours on chain, once, and a share of the launchpad fee on each of their curve trades there is credited to your code from then on.\n\n  \n\nWithdraw\n\nThe same page shows what you have earned and the volume you have referred, with a \nWithdraw\n button that sends your earnings to your wallet in the chain's currency.\n\nReferral rates\n\n  \n\n20%\n\nof the launchpad's share of each fee\n\n  \n\n30%\n\nonce your code has referred 30 ETH of trades\n\nThe launchpad's share is the half of the 1% curve fee that does not go to the creator, so a referrer earns 0.1% of each referred trade, rising to 0.15%. Referral earnings come from curve trades only, and are not taken from the creator's half. Once a token graduates its trades happen in the Uniswap pool, which pays no referral share. See \nToken Launchpad\n for the whole fee split.\n\nLaunches are coming soon.\n New tokens cannot be launched on creora.ai yet, but codes can be registered now and earn on the curve trades of tokens already launched.",
  "revenue": "Own and earn\n\nPayments and Revenue\n\nCreora is built so that what you create can earn. Today you own what you build, a token you launch pays you half of its curve fees, and referrals pay you on chain. Payments inside your apps and automating how revenue moves are planned.\n\nOwnership\n\nThe apps you build on Creora belong to you. You can download any project as a zip with nothing tying it to Creora, push it to your own GitHub, and serve it on your own domain. Token rewards and referral earnings are credited to you on chain and can only be paid to your address, and Creora never holds your key.\n\nWays to earn\n\n  \n\nCreator rewards\n\n0.5% of every curve trade of your site's token, claimable at any time. Launching a new token on creora.ai is coming soon.\n\nToken Launchpad \n\n  \n\nReferrals\n\nA share of the launchpad fee on curve trades by the people you bring in.\n\nReferrals \n\n  \n\nPlanned\n\nApp payments\n\nBuilt in payments for the apps you make will come later. Today an app can already reach a payment provider's API through a connection, which keeps your key on the server.\n\n  \n\nPlanned\n\nAgent earnings\n\nYou will earn 70% of the price you set when people run an agent you publish. Your own agents work for you today; publishing them is planned.\n\nAgents \n\nRevenue settlement\n\nWhat you earn from the launchpad is settled on chain as each trade happens, so it reaches its owner without waiting for a payout. A creator's reward stays in the token's curve contract until you claim it, and referral earnings wait in the launchpad's referral contract until you withdraw them, both in the chain's currency.\n\n  \n\nSource\n\nA curve trade\n\n  \n\n  \n\nSettle\n\nCredited on chain\n\n  \n\n  \n\nOwner\n\nYour wallet, when you claim\n\nPaying for Creora\n\nYou pay for Creora itself in dollar stablecoins (USDC, USDT or USDC.e on Arbitrum, USDG or USDC on Robinhood Chain, or USDT or USDC on Ethereum) or in Dash. Your account has a deposit address of its own under \nSettings › Billing\n, and whatever arrives there becomes credits by itself, a thousand to the dollar. For Dash, \nPay with Dash\n gives your account a Dash address of its own, which works the same way. Plans, top ups and prices are on \nCredits and API key\n.\n\nRevenue automation\n\nPlanned\n\nAutomating how revenue moves, from your apps and agents to the people who own them, is planned, and so is settling payments across the ecosystem in $CREORA. Neither exists today, and there is no $CREORA token yet; see \nToken Utility\n.",
  "tokens": "Own and earn\n\nToken Launchpad\n\nRobinhood Chain\n\nArbitrum\n\nNew launches coming soon\n\nEvery site you build can have one token of its own. Each launch is a fair launch on a price curve that graduates into a Uniswap pool, and the creator earns half of every curve fee.\n\nLaunching opens soon.\n On creora.ai the launch buttons are marked Coming soon for now and the form is not offered yet. Tokens already launched keep their pages and trade as usual, and the contracts are live on both chains.\n\nHow a fair launch works\n\n  \n\n1 billion\n\ntokens minted once, and never again\n\n  \n\n80%\n\nof supply sold on the price curve\n\n  \n\n1%\n\nfee on each curve buy and sell\n\n  \n\n0.5%\n\nof each curve trade credited to the creator\n\nThe whole supply is minted once and 80% of it goes onto a curve that sells to everybody at the same price from the first second. Nobody receives tokens before anybody else, and the creator's own first buy happens inside the transaction that creates the curve, at the same price the next buyer pays. Each buy moves the price up a little and each sell moves it back down by the same rule.\n\n  \n\nLaunch\n\nToken created for your site\n\n  \n\n  \n\nThe curve\n\nEveryone buys at one price\n\n  \n\n  \n\nRaise fills\n\nCurve closes for good\n\n  \n\n  \n\nGraduation\n\nUniswap pool at the closing price\n\nWhen the curve raises its target it closes permanently and becomes a Uniswap pool, opened at the closing price and filled with the raise and the unsold tokens. Trading then continues in the pool.\n\nChains and costs\n\nA token lives on the chain it was launched on, and every figure on its page is in that chain's currency. The launch form reads each chain's cost and raise directly from the chain before you choose.\n\n  \n\nRobinhood Chain\n\nCurrency\n\nETH\n\nCreate\n\n0.0005 ETH\n\nRaise\n\n~3.96 ETH\n\nMax first buy\n\n20 ETH\n\n  \n\nArbitrum\n\nCurrency\n\nETH\n\nCreate\n\n0.0005 ETH\n\nRaise\n\n~3.96 ETH\n\nMax first buy\n\n20 ETH\n\nThe creation cost goes to the launchpad's treasury and your wallet pays network gas on top. Gas on either chain is a fraction of a cent. The launchpad is the same contract at the same address on both chains, and creora.ai offers both.\n\nLaunching a token\n\nLaunching is coming soon on creora.ai, so the steps below are how the form works once it opens. You will need a wallet on the chain you choose with a little ETH in it. Every transaction is signed by your own wallet, and Creora never holds your key or your money.\n\nStart from the coin button in the editor's top bar, which fills the form from the site in front of you, or from \nTokens › Launch a token\n, which first asks which of your sites the token is for. A site can have only one token, and cancelling in your wallet costs nothing.\n\n  \n\nChoose the site and chain\n\nEach chain card shows what a launch there costs and what its curve raises.\n\n  \n\nName and ticker\n\nThe ticker is 2 to 12 capital letters and digits.\n\n  \n\nPicture and description\n\nA square picture is optional. The description appears on the token page and is written on chain as the token's own description.\n\n  \n\nFirst buy and links\n\nOptionally buy some of your token in the launch transaction at the opening price, up to 20 ETH or 50,000 USDC, and add Website, X, Telegram, Discord, Farcaster or GitHub links.\n\n  \n\nLaunch\n\nPress Launch and confirm in your wallet. If the wallet is on another network it is asked to switch first, and the token page opens once the chain confirms.\n\nThe token page and trading\n\nThe top of the page shows \nProgress to graduation\n as a bar of the amount raised against the target. The chart draws candles and volume from real trades, switchable between Price and MCap with candles from 1m to 1d. \nTrades\n lists each trade with its side, amounts, venue and trader, and \nHolders\n lists the largest holders with the curve, pool and creator labelled.\n\nSigned in with a wallet, you can buy with presets of 0.01, 0.05, 0.1 and 0.5 ETH and sell 25%, 50% or all of your tokens. Every trade carries a 2% price floor, so the chain refuses a trade rather than filling it worse than quoted, and a first sell needs one approval signature. After graduation the same box trades in the pool. Every confirmation links to the transaction on Blockscout.\n\nWhere the fee goes\n\nThe token itself has no tax, so sending it between wallets costs only gas. The only fee is the 1% the curve takes on each buy and sell, which is split on chain as the trade happens. How it splits depends on whether the trader came through a referral link.\n\nA trade with no referrer\n\n  \n\n50%\n\nCreator\n\n  \n\n50%\n\nTreasury\n\nA trade by someone a referrer brought in\n\n  \n\n50%\n\nCreator\n\n  \n\n10 to 15%\n\nReferrer\n\n  \n\n35 to 40%\n\nTreasury\n\nHalf always goes to the creator. The other half goes to the launchpad treasury operated by Creora, except when the trader was referred: then 20% of that half goes to the referrer, rising to 30% once the referrer's code has brought 30 ETH of trades. That is 10% of the whole fee, rising to 15%, and the treasury keeps the rest. See \nReferrals\n.\n\nThe creator's share is credited on chain in the chain's currency and appears on the token page, when signed in with the launching wallet, as \nYour reward\n with a \nClaim reward\n button. Claim whenever you like, as often as you like. It never expires and can only be paid to your address.\n\nAfter graduation the curve and its fee are gone. The pool charges a 1% swap fee, which goes to the holder of the liquidity position, and that is the launchpad operator. The pool runs without hooks. The creator earns nothing further from trading at that point.\n\nIf something goes wrong\n\n  \n\nThe wallet says you are on the wrong network\n\nApprove the switch to the chain the token is on, which the message names, and try again.\n\n  \n\nYou cancelled by accident\n\nNothing was spent. Press the button again.\n\n  \n\nThe chain could not be read\n\nThe figures are live reads and a read can fail. Press the refresh button at the top right, or reload.\n\n  \n\nA buy filled for less than you asked\n\nThe curve had nearly run out, so it sold you what was left and the money it did not need came straight back.\n\nUnderstand the risks.\n Token prices can fall quickly and a token can lose all of its value. Liquidity in a graduated pool is held by Creora's operator and is not locked, and every token page says who holds it. Tokens are created by their creators and are not endorsed by Creora, Robinhood, Circle or anyone else. The contracts cannot be changed once deployed and their verified source is published on each chain's explorer. Nothing here is financial advice.",
  "token_utility": "$CREORA\n\nToken Utility\n\nPlanned\n\n$CREORA is planned as the single token that will connect AI creation on Creora to on chain economic activity. It is meant to be used at each point where value moves through the platform, from the first prompt to the settlement of revenue.\n\nNot available yet.\n There is no $CREORA token today, and nothing on this page can be bought, held or staked. Creora is paid for in credits, which you buy with dollar stablecoins or Dash; see \nCredits and API key\n.\n\nWhat $CREORA will be used for\n\n  \n\nPlatform access and AI usage\n\nAccess to the workspace and usage of the models for chat, generation and building.\n\n  \n\nApplication deployment\n\nDeploying and publishing the applications you build on Creora.\n\n  \n\nAgent activation and execution\n\nActivating agents and paying for the work they carry out. \nAgents\n run on credits today.\n\n  \n\nPayments and revenue settlement\n\nPayments between users, apps and agents across the ecosystem, and settlement of the revenue they earn.\n\n  \n\nStaking and governance\n\nStaking to take part in decisions about how the protocol develops; see \nStaking and Governance\n.\n\nHow value will move\n\nThe plan is for builders to spend $CREORA to use models, deploy apps and run agents. The apps and agents they create would earn from their users, and that revenue would settle back to the builders who own them. Holders who stake the token would help decide how the protocol evolves.\n\n  \n\nUse\n\nAI usage and deployment\n\n  \n\n  \n\nRun\n\nApps and agents\n\n  \n\n  \n\nEarn\n\nRevenue settlement\n\n  \n\n  \n\nGovern\n\nStaking\n\n$CREORA and credits\n\nUsage inside the workspace is measured in credits, where one credit is a thousandth of a dollar of AI work. Every account gets three free uses a day, and credits come with a plan or a top up paid in dollar stablecoins or Dash. The \nCredits and API key\n page covers current pricing. $CREORA is meant to tie that usage, along with deployment, agents and settlement, to the on chain economy.\n\nBeware of copies.\n Since there is no $CREORA token yet, any token using the name today is not Creora's. If one is launched, trust only a contract address published through \ncreora.ai\n and \n@CreoraAI_RH\n. Tokens launched by users on the launchpad are not affiliated with Creora either."
};
